var e=JSON.parse('[[1,"The basic structure of an HTML page begins with `<!DOCTYPE html>` to declare the document type. Then comes the `<html>` tag, inside which there are two key sections: `<head>` (for meta information, styles and links to resources) and `<body>` (for the content the user sees). For example:\\n\\n`<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n<title>My first page</title>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n<h1>Hello, world!</h1>\\n</body>\\n</html>`","HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is used to create the structure of web pages. Every HTML page begins with the document type declaration `<!DOCTYPE html>`, which tells the browser that the latest version of HTML (HTML5) is being used.\\nThe page\'s structure is organized into tags:\\n\\n- `<html>`: the page\'s root element. All the HTML code it contains sits inside it.\\n\\n- `<head>`: the section for meta information. This is where the following go:\\n\\n- `<title>`: the page\'s title, shown in the browser tab.\\n\\n- `<meta>`: meta tags, for stating the character encoding, for example (`<meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">`).\\n\\n- Linking styles (`<link>`) or scripts (`<script>`).\\n\\n- `<body>`: the section containing the page\'s visible content — text, images, buttons and so on.\\n\\nAn example of the basic structure:\\n\\n`<meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">` sets the UTF-8 encoding so that text displays correctly.\\n`<meta name=\\"viewport\\" content=\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\\">` helps adapt the page for mobile devices.\\n\\nCreating the page\'s basic structure is the first step when developing a web application. For example, if you want to build a business card or a landing page, you will start from the structure described above. It also serves as the foundation for adding styles (CSS) and logic (JavaScript).","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n  <head>\\n    <meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">\\n    <meta name=\\"viewport\\" content=\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\\">\\n    <title>A basic HTML page</title>\\n  </head>\\n  <body>\\n    <h1>Welcome!</h1>\\n    <p>This is an example of the basic HTML structure.</p>\\n  </body>\\n</html>"],[2,"Meta tags are elements in the `<head>` of an HTML document that carry meta information — that is, data about the page itself. They are not shown to the user directly, but they affect indexing in search engines, the correct display of the page and interaction with social networks.\\nFor example, `<meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">` sets the encoding, while `<meta name=\\"description\\" content=\\"A description of the page\\">` gives search engines a description of the page.","Meta tags (from the word \\"meta information\\") are special tags placed inside the `<head>` section of an HTML document that give browsers, search engines and other services information about the page.\\nThe main kinds of meta tag:\\n\\n- The encoding:\\n\\nThis tag sets the character encoding so that text displays correctly. UTF-8 supports most languages and characters.\\n\\n- Adaptation for mobile devices:\\n\\nThis tag helps the page adapt to the size of the screen, especially on mobile devices.\\n\\n- A description of the page:\\n\\nUsed to improve SEO (search engine optimization). Search engines often show this description in their results.\\n\\n- Keywords:\\n\\nRarely used nowadays, since search engines rely more on the page\'s content.\\n\\n- The author:\\n\\nStates the page\'s author.\\n\\n- Social networks:\\nFor the correct display of a link on social media, for example:\\n\\nThese tags are used by Facebook, Twitter and other platforms to build an attractive card when a link is posted.\\n\\nAn example of use:\\n\\nSEO: helps search engines index the page better.\\nMobile devices: makes the site comfortable to view on different screens.\\nSocial networks: improves how a link looks when it is posted.\\nMeta tags matter at every stage of development, from building landing pages to complex web applications.","<meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">\\n\\n<meta name=\\"viewport\\" content=\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\\">\\n\\n<meta name=\\"description\\" content=\\"This is a description of the page for search engines.\\">\\n\\n<meta name=\\"keywords\\" content=\\"HTML, meta tags, frontend\\">\\n\\n<meta name=\\"author\\" content=\\"The author\'s name\\">\\n\\n<meta property=\\"og:title\\" content=\\"The page\'s title\\">\\n<meta property=\\"og:description\\" content=\\"A description for social networks\\">\\n<meta property=\\"og:image\\" content=\\"link_to_image\\">\\n\\n<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n  <head>\\n    <meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">\\n    <meta name=\\"viewport\\" content=\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\\">\\n    <meta name=\\"description\\" content=\\"A tutorial page about meta tags.\\">\\n    <meta name=\\"author\\" content=\\"John Smith\\">\\n    <title>Meta tags in HTML</title>\\n  </head>\\n  <body>\\n    <h1>An example of using meta tags</h1>\\n  </body>\\n</html>"],[3,"The `<link>` tag is used to attach external resources to an HTML document. Most often it is applied to link CSS styles. The tag sits inside the `<head>` section and is not displayed on the page.\\nFor example:\\n\\n`<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\">\\n`\\nThis code attaches the style file `styles.css` to the page.","The `<link>` tag is an HTML element that lets you attach external resources to a web page. It is used only in the `<head>` section and is not displayed on the page itself.\\nThe main attributes of the `<link>` tag:\\n• `rel` (relationship): states the kind of relationship between the current document and the resource being attached.\\n• `stylesheet` — for attaching CSS files.\\n• `icon` — for specifying a favicon.\\n• `href` (hyperlink reference): the path to the file being attached. This may be a relative or an absolute URL.\\n• `type` (optional): the resource\'s MIME type, `text/css` for example.\\nExamples of use:\\n\\n- Attaching a CSS file:\\n\\nThis is the most widespread use of the `<link>` tag. It links an external style file to the HTML document.\\n\\n- Specifying a favicon:\\n\\nThis tag tells the browser which icon to show on the page\'s tab.\\n\\n- Attaching other resources:\\nAttaching fonts, for example:\\n\\nThe full example:\\n\\nThe `<link>` tag lets you separate a web page\'s structure from its styles. This is convenient for managing styles (CSS) and improves performance, since browsers can cache the attached files. For example, when developing a site, attaching fonts and CSS through `<link>` helps you change the appearance quickly without touching the HTML.","<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\">\\n\\n<link rel=\\"icon\\" href=\\"favicon.ico\\" type=\\"image/x-icon\\">\\n\\n<link href=\\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Roboto&display=swap\\" rel=\\"stylesheet\\">\\n\\n<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n  <head>\\n    <meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">\\n    <meta name=\\"viewport\\" content=\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\\">\\n    <title>An example with the link tag</title>\\n    <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\">\\n    <link rel=\\"icon\\" href=\\"favicon.ico\\" type=\\"image/x-icon\\">\\n  </head>\\n  <body>\\n    <h1>An example of attaching resources</h1>\\n  </body>\\n</html>"],[4,"An attribute in HTML is additional information that sets an element\'s properties. It is written inside the opening tag in the form name=\\"value\\".\\nFor example, the href attribute on an <a> tag sets the link, while src on an <img> gives the path to an image. Attributes affect the appearance or functionality of elements, but their values are not visible on the page itself.\\nFor example:\\n\\n`<a href=\\"https://example.com\\">Go to the site</a>`","Attributes in HTML give elements additional information, helping you configure their properties or behaviour. Attributes are written in an element\'s opening tag and consist of a name and a value separated by an `=` sign.\\nThe main principles:\\n\\n- Attributes are stated only in the opening tag.\\n\\n- Attribute values are enclosed in quotes (double or single).\\n\\n- A single element may have several attributes, which are listed separated by spaces.\\n\\nExamples of popular attributes:\\n\\n- `href` — for links:\\n\\nStates the address the link leads to.\\n\\n- `src` — for images:\\n\\nStates the path to the image.\\n\\n- `alt` — alternative text for an image:\\n\\nShown if the image fails to load.\\n\\n- `id` and `class` — for identification and styling:\\n\\n`id` must be unique on the page, while `class` is used to group elements.\\n\\n- `disabled` — a boolean attribute that makes an element inactive:\\n\\nAn example with several attributes:\\n\\n- `href`: the link\'s address.\\n\\n- `target=\\"_blank\\"`: opening in a new tab.\\n\\n- `title`: a tooltip on hover.\\n\\nPractical use:\\nAttributes are used to control the behaviour and display of HTML elements. For example, you can configure a button so that it is inactive (the `disabled` attribute), add styles through `class` or attach external resources through `src` and `href`.\\nA practical example:\\n\\nAttributes make elements flexible and functional, which lets you build complex, interactive web pages.","<a href=\\"https://example.com\\">Go</a>\\n\\n<img src=\\"image.jpg\\" alt=\\"A description of the image\\">\\n\\n<img src=\\"image.jpg\\" alt=\\"A description of the image\\">\\n\\n<div id=\\"main\\" class=\\"container\\"></div>\\n\\n<button disabled>A button</button>\\n\\n<a href=\\"https://example.com\\" target=\\"_blank\\" title=\\"Will open in a new window\\">A link</a>\\n\\n<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n  <head>\\n    <title>An example of attributes</title>\\n  </head>\\n  <body>\\n    <h1 id=\\"main-title\\" class=\\"title\\">A heading</h1>\\n    <a href=\\"https://example.com\\" target=\\"_blank\\">Go to the site</a>\\n    <img src=\\"image.jpg\\" alt=\\"A picture\\">\\n  </body>\\n</html>"],[5,"To create a telephone link you use the `<a>` tag with an `href` attribute whose value begins with `tel:`.\\nFor example:\\n\\n`<a href=\\"tel:+1234567890\\">Call</a>`\\nWhen the user taps this link, the device offers to place a call to the given number automatically. This is especially useful for mobile users.","A telephone link in HTML is a hyperlink that uses the `tel:` scheme to specify a phone number. Such a link lets a call be initiated on click, provided the device supports that function (a smartphone, for example, or a computer with telephony software installed).\\nHow a telephone link works:\\n\\n- The link\'s format:\\nThe `href` attribute contains the phone number in the form `tel:number`. The number can be written with the country code so that it works universally.\\nFor example:\\n\\n- Support for additional characters:\\nYou can add characters:\\n\\n- Spaces, dashes and brackets are ignored by the device.\\n\\n- For pauses or additional actions, commas `,` (a pause) or semicolons `;` (wait) are used.\\n\\n- The link\'s text:\\nThe text between the `<a>` tags is visible to the user and can be anything you like.\\n\\nThe full example:\\n\\nPractical use:\\n\\n- Mobile sites: convenient for users who want to contact a company quickly.\\n\\n- Customer support: on contact pages telephone links can be used to improve the user experience.\\n\\nTips:\\n\\n- Give the phone number in international format so that the link works on any device.\\n\\n- Make sure the link\'s text is clear and motivates the user to act (\\"Call\\", for example).\\n\\n- Telephone links improve a site\'s usability, especially for mobile users, and help you interact with your audience directly.","<a href=\\"tel:+1234567890\\">Call</a>\\n\\n<a href=\\"tel:+1234567890,123\\">Call with an extension</a>\\n\\n<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n  <head>\\n    <title>A telephone link</title>\\n  </head>\\n  <body>\\n    <p>To contact us, tap:</p>\\n    <a href=\\"tel:+1234567890\\">Call us</a>\\n  </body>\\n</html>"],[6,"A browser is a program that downloads HTML, CSS, JavaScript and other resources from a web server, interprets them and displays the page for the user. It works in stages:\\n\\n- Loading: fetching the resources (HTML, CSS, JS, images) over HTTP/HTTPS.\\n\\n- Parsing: the HTML is turned into a DOM tree and the CSS into a CSSOM.\\n\\n- Rendering: the DOM and CSSOM are combined into a render tree, which is used to display the elements on the screen.\\n\\n- JavaScript: scripts are executed, and they may change the page (updating the DOM, for example).\\n\\n- Painting: the browser calculates where the elements go (layout) and draws them (paint).","A browser\'s work can be divided into several key stages, each of which plays its own part in creating and displaying a web page.\\n\\n- Loading the resources\\n\\nWhen you type a URL in the address bar:\\n\\n- The browser sends a request to the server over the HTTP/HTTPS protocols.\\n\\n- The server returns an HTML document.\\n\\n- The browser analyses the HTML, finds links to other resources (CSS, JavaScript, images) and loads them.\\n\\n- Parsing the HTML and creating the DOM\\n\\nThe HTML document is broken down into elements and nodes, from which the Document Object Model (DOM) is built — the page\'s hierarchical structure.\\nAn example DOM for some HTML:\\n\\nThe DOM structure:\\n\\n- Parsing the CSS and creating the CSSOM\\n\\nThe loaded styles are turned into the CSS Object Model (CSSOM), which represents the elements\' styles.\\nFor example:\\n\\nThe CSSOM contains the rules for every element.\\n\\n- Creating the render tree\\n\\nThe DOM and CSSOM are combined into a render tree, which contains only the page\'s visible elements with their styles. Invisible elements (those with `display: none`, for example) are excluded.\\n\\n- Calculating the layout\\n\\nThe browser works out where on the screen the elements should go — the height and width of blocks, the position of text and images, for example.\\n\\n- Painting\\n\\nThe browser draws the elements on the screen taking their styles, colours and shadows into account.\\n\\n- Processing the JavaScript\\n\\nJavaScript is executed in an engine (V8 for Chrome, for example). Scripts can change the DOM or the CSSOM, which sometimes triggers a layout recalculation or a repaint.\\nFor example:\\n\\n- Optimization through compositing\\n\\nModern browsers use compositing to separate layers (animations, for example) and process them independently, which speeds rendering up.\\nThe browser\'s flow of work (simplified):\\n\\n- Receive the HTML → parse → DOM\\n\\n- Receive the CSS → parse → CSSOM\\n\\n- DOM + CSSOM → render tree → layout → paint\\n\\n- Display the page to the user\\n\\nAn example of the resources interacting:\\nHTML:\\n\\nCSS (`styles.css`):\\n\\nJavaScript (`script.js`):\\n\\nOptimizing the browser\'s work:\\n\\n- Minimize the number of requests: combine CSS and JS files.\\n\\n- Use asynchronous loading: for JavaScript, add the `async` or `defer` attribute.\\n\\n- Minify your files: reduce the size of the resources.\\n\\n- Avoid blocking JavaScript and CSS: this speeds up the display of the page.\\n\\nA browser is a complex system that does an enormous amount of work in milliseconds in order to display a web page. Understanding how it works helps you build fast, optimized web applications.","<html>\\n  <body>\\n    <h1>Hello, world!</h1>\\n  </body>\\n</html>\\n\\n<html>\\n └── <body>\\n      └── <h1>\\n           └── \\"Hello, world!\\"\\n\\nbody {\\n  font-size: 16px;\\n}\\nh1 {\\n  color: red;\\n}\\n\\ndocument.querySelector(\\"h1\\").style.color = \\"blue\\";\\n\\n<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n  <head>\\n    <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\">\\n  </head>\\n  <body>\\n    <h1>Hello, world!</h1>\\n    <script src=\\"script.js\\"><\/script>\\n  </body>\\n</html>\\n\\nh1 {\\n  color: red;\\n}\\n\\ndocument.querySelector(\\"h1\\").textContent = \\"Hello, JavaScript!\\";"],[7,"- Parsing the HTML\\n\\n- The browser starts parsing the HTML document and building the DOM tree (Document Object Model), which represents the hierarchical structure of all the page\'s elements.\\n\\n- If the browser finds a `<script>`, it pauses parsing, loads and executes the script, which may change the DOM\'s structure.\\n\\n- Loading external resources\\n\\n- While parsing the HTML the browser discovers links to external resources (CSS, images, fonts, scripts) and starts loading them in parallel.\\n\\n- The CSS is loaded and parsed, forming the CSSOM (CSS Object Model).\\n\\n- Building the render tree\\n\\n- The DOM and CSSOM are combined to create the render tree, which is the structure of the elements to be displayed on the screen with their styles applied.\\n\\n- Calculating the geometry (Layout)\\n\\n- The browser calculates the size and position of every element on the page based on the render tree. This process is called layout or reflow.\\n\\n- Painting\\n\\n- The browser turns the render tree into pixels on the screen, drawing text, colours, images, shadows and other graphical elements.\\n\\n- Compositing\\n\\n- Some elements may be painted in separate layers to improve performance (animations or fixed elements, for example). At the compositing stage the browser combines those layers into a single image for display on the screen.\\n\\n- The user interface (UI)\\n\\n- The browser handles user events (clicks, scrolling, data entry and so on).\\n\\n- JavaScript can change the DOM, which may trigger the layout and painting stages again.","- Parsing the HTML\\n\\n- The browser starts parsing the HTML document and building the DOM tree (Document Object Model), which represents the hierarchical structure of all the page\'s elements.\\n\\n- If the browser finds a `<script>`, it pauses parsing, loads and executes the script, which may change the DOM\'s structure.\\n\\n- Loading external resources\\n\\n- While parsing the HTML the browser discovers links to external resources (CSS, images, fonts, scripts) and starts loading them in parallel.\\n\\n- The CSS is loaded and parsed, forming the CSSOM (CSS Object Model).\\n\\n- Building the render tree\\n\\n- The DOM and CSSOM are combined to create the render tree, which is the structure of the elements to be displayed on the screen with their styles applied.\\n\\n- Calculating the geometry (Layout)\\n\\n- The browser calculates the size and position of every element on the page based on the render tree. This process is called layout or reflow.\\n\\n- Painting\\n\\n- The browser turns the render tree into pixels on the screen, drawing text, colours, images, shadows and other graphical elements.\\n\\n- Compositing\\n\\n- Some elements may be painted in separate layers to improve performance (animations or fixed elements, for example). At the compositing stage the browser combines those layers into a single image for display on the screen.\\n\\n- The user interface (UI)\\n\\n- The browser handles user events (clicks, scrolling, data entry and so on).\\n\\n- JavaScript can change the DOM, which may trigger the layout and painting stages again.",null],[8,"There are three main ways of attaching CSS to HTML: inline, internal and external. Inline CSS applies styles directly to an element using the `style` attribute. Internal CSS is placed inside a `<style>` tag in the `<head>` of the HTML document and applies to every element on the page. External CSS lives in a separate file with the `.css` extension, which is attached to the HTML through a `<link>` tag in the `<head>`, which lets the same styles be used on several pages.","HTML has three main ways of attaching CSS, each with its own advantages and used in different situations.\\nInline CSS: this method involves using the `style` attribute right inside an HTML element. For example:\\n`<h1 style=\\"color: blue;\\">Hello, world!</h1>`\\nInline CSS is convenient for applying styles to individual elements quickly, but it can lead to inefficient code if used too often.\\nInternal CSS: the styles are defined inside a `<style>` tag placed in the document\'s `<head>`. For example:\\n\\nInternal CSS is convenient when you want the styles to apply to one page only. It is not suitable for large projects, however, since it requires duplicating the code on different pages.\\nExternal CSS: this method means creating a separate CSS file, which is attached to the HTML document with a `<link>` tag. For example:\\n\\nExternal CSS is the most preferable method for large projects, since it lets you separate the HTML structure from the styles. This makes maintaining and changing styles easier, because it is enough to make a change in one place and it applies to every page that uses that file.\\nEach of these methods can be useful depending on the context. In most cases external CSS is recommended for organizing code and improving its readability.","<head>\\n  <style>\\n    body {\\n      background-color: lightgray;\\n    }\\n    h1 {\\n      color: green;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n</head>\\n\\n<head><link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\"></head>"],[9,"`<!DOCTYPE>` is an instruction to the browser telling it which mode it should process the HTML code in. Without it the browser may work in compatibility mode (quirks mode), which can make the page display incorrectly. Modern pages normally use `<!DOCTYPE html>` to switch on standards mode, which supports modern web technologies.\\nFor example:\\n\\n`<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n<title>An example</title>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n<h1>Hello, world!</h1>\\n</body>\\n</html>`","`<!DOCTYPE>` is an instruction to the browser telling it which version of the HTML specification to use when processing the page. It is not an HTML tag and is not displayed in the page\'s interface.\\nWeb pages can be displayed differently depending on whether standards mode or compatibility mode is switched on:\\n\\n- Standards mode: the browser renders the page in accordance with modern HTML and CSS standards.\\n\\n- Compatibility mode (quirks mode): the browser imitates obsolete behaviour for old web pages.\\n\\nWhy does this matter?\\nIf the `<!DOCTYPE>` declaration is absent, browsers may interpret the page\'s code incorrectly, especially when CSS is used. The size of blocks and the handling of tables, for example, may differ.\\nFor example:\\nA minimal HTML page:\\n\\nThe history of `<!DOCTYPE>`\\nHTML 4.01:\\nThe declaration was more complicated, since it included references to a DTD (Document Type Definition). For example:\\n\\nThis told the browser to use strict mode without obsolete elements.\\nHTML5:\\nThe simplified form `<!DOCTYPE html>`, since HTML5 no longer uses a DTD.\\n\\nHow does `<!DOCTYPE>` work?\\nWhen the browser sees the `<!DOCTYPE>` declaration, it:\\n\\n- Switches to standards mode.\\n\\n- Interprets the HTML and CSS in accordance with modern specifications.\\n\\nIf `<!DOCTYPE>` is absent, the browser switches on compatibility mode, where obsolete behaviour is supported. This can lead to visual and functional errors.\\n\\nA practical example:\\nWithout `<!DOCTYPE>`:\\n\\n- The browser may interpret the width of a block with `width: 100px;` as including its margin and border.\\nWith `<!DOCTYPE>`:\\n\\n- The browser interprets only the block\'s content in accordance with the CSS box model.\\n\\n`<!DOCTYPE>` is needed so that the browser renders the page correctly in modern standards mode. It is a mandatory part of any HTML document and lays the foundation for your site to display and work properly.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html lang=\\"en\\">\\n<head>\\n  <meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">\\n  <title>A document</title>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <h1>An example page</h1>\\n</body>\\n</html>\\n\\n<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC \\"-//W3C//DTD HTML 4.01//EN\\" \\"http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/strict.dtd\\">"],[10,"HTML has three main kinds of list: ordered lists (`<ol>`), unordered lists (`<ul>`) and definition lists (`<dl>`). Ordered lists use numbers or letters to mark each item, unordered ones use bullets, and definition lists contain \\"term-description\\" pairs.","HTML provides three kinds of list for structuring data:\\nAn ordered list (`<ol>`) — used when the order of the items matters. The list\'s items are enclosed in `<li>` tags, and the list as a whole in `<ol>`. By default the items are numbered with digits, but the style can be changed to Roman numerals or letters through the `type` attribute.\\n\\nAn unordered list (`<ul>`) — used when the order of the items does not matter. In this list the items are also enclosed in `<li>`, but bullets (dots, squares) are used instead of numbers.\\n\\nA definition list (`<dl>`) — used to create \\"term-description\\" pairs. The terms are enclosed in a `<dt>` tag and the descriptions in `<dd>`.\\n\\nThese lists are used to organize information on pages, improving their readability and accessibility.","<ol>\\n  <li>The first item</li>\\n  <li>The second item</li>\\n  <li>The third item</li> \\n</ol>\\n\\n<ul>  \\n  <li>Item 1</li>  \\n  <li>Item 2</li>  \\n  <li>Item 3</li> \\n</ul>\\n\\n<dl>  \\n  <dt>HTML</dt>  \\n  <dd>HyperText Markup Language</dd>  \\n  <dt>CSS</dt>  \\n  <dd>Cascading Style Sheets</dd> \\n</dl>"],[11,"To embed video in HTML the `<video>` tag is used. Inside it the video\'s sources are given through `<source>` tags, and you can also add attributes such as `controls` to display the playback controls, `autoplay` for automatic playback and `loop` for repeated playback.","HTML5 added support for video without any need for third-party plugins such as Flash. The `<video>` tag lets you embed video files in a page.\\nIt supports several attributes for controlling the video\'s behaviour:\\n\\n- `controls` — shows the control panel (play, pause, seek).\\n\\n- `autoplay` — starts playing the video automatically when the page loads.\\n\\n- `loop` — makes the video play in a loop.\\n\\n- `muted` — turns the video\'s sound off by default.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\nInside the `<video>` tag you can give several sources (`<source>`) in order to support different video formats in different browsers. If the browser does not support HTML5 video, the fallback text is displayed.","<video width=\\"400\\" controls>  \\n  <source src=\\"video.mp4\\" type=\\"video/mp4\\">  \\n  <source src=\\"video.ogg\\" type=\\"video/ogg\\">  Your browser does not support video. \\n</video>"],[12,"To embed audio in HTML the `<audio>` tag is used. As with video, you can give several audio file formats through `<source>` tags, and the `controls`, `autoplay` and `loop` attributes control playback.","The `<audio>` tag in HTML5 lets you embed audio files in a web page. As with video, you can use attributes to control the audio file\'s behaviour:\\n\\n- `controls` — adds a control panel for the user.\\n\\n- `autoplay` — starts playing the audio automatically.\\n\\n- `loop` — repeats the audio file in a loop.\\n\\n- `muted` — mutes the audio by default.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\nThis code embeds an audio file with playback controls. As with video, several `<source>` elements let you support various audio formats for different browsers.","<audio controls>  \\n  <source src=\\"audio.mp3\\" type=\\"audio/mpeg\\">  \\n  <source src=\\"audio.ogg\\" type=\\"audio/ogg\\">  \\n  Your browser does not support audio. \\n</audio>"],[13,"The `<iframe>` tag is used to embed content from another web resource in a page — another site, a YouTube video or a map, for example. It creates a window inside the current page where the external resource is loaded. `<iframe>` is useful for integrating third-party content while preserving the page\'s main structure.","The `<iframe>` tag (from \\"inline frame\\") lets you insert another document into a web page — the content of another web page, a video or a map, for example. This creates a window inside the current page where the external resource is loaded. It is used to embed videos from YouTube, maps from Google Maps, documents and other sites.\\nFor example:\\n\\nThis code embeds the site `example.com` in your page. It is important to bear in mind that not all sites allow embedding through an `<iframe>` for security reasons (some sites use the `X-Frame-Options` header as protection, for example).\\n\\nThe `width` and `height` attributes set the window\'s dimensions.\\n\\nThe main advantage of using an `<iframe>` is the ability to integrate external content without changing the page\'s main code. You should be careful with it, however, because of the potential security vulnerabilities involved in loading external content.","<iframe src=\\"https://www.example.com\\" width=\\"600\\" height=\\"400\\"></iframe>"],[14,"To create links in HTML the `<a>` tag is used. It has an `href` attribute that sets the URL the link will lead to. Inside the `<a>` tag goes the text or other element the user can click to follow the link.","The `<a>` tag (anchor) is used to create hyperlinks in HTML documents. This tag connects text or an element on the page to another document, file or web page. Its main attribute is `href`, which gives the path to the resource the link leads to.\\nFor example:\\n\\nHere, when the text \\"Go to the site\\" is clicked, the browser navigates to the given URL. With the `target=\\"_blank\\"` attribute you can open the link in a new tab, and `rel=\\"noopener noreferrer\\"` is added to improve security when `target=\\"_blank\\"` is used.\\nThe `<a>` tag can also be used to create anchor links within a single page. This is done by setting an identifier (`id`) on the element you want to link to and using `href` with that identifier.\\nAn example of an anchor link:","<a href=\\"https://www.example.com\\">Go to the site</a>\\n\\n<a href=\\"#section1\\">Go to section 1</a> \\n<div id=\\"section1\\">This is section 1</div>"],[15,"The `<picture>` tag lets you specify several image sources for different conditions, such as screen width or image format, so that the browser can pick the most suitable one. The `<img>` tag is used to insert a single image simply. The difference is that `<picture>` gives you more flexibility for responsive images.","The `<picture>` tag in HTML is used to provide several versions of the same image, so that the browser can choose the most suitable one depending on conditions such as screen size or support for image formats. This is especially useful for responsive sites, when images of different size or quality are needed on different devices. Inside a `<picture>` tag there may be several `<source>` elements with `media` and `type` attributes that set the conditions for choosing an image. If none of the conditions is met, the image given in the `<img>` tag is displayed — and that tag is mandatory inside `<picture>`.\\nFor example:\\n\\nHere, if the screen is wider than 800 pixels, the image `large.jpg` is loaded. If the screen is wider than 400 pixels but narrower than 800, `medium.jpg` is loaded. In all other cases `small.jpg` is used. The `<img>` tag, unlike `<picture>`, simply displays one image and cannot adapt to various conditions.\\nUsing `<picture>` lets you improve a site\'s performance by loading smaller images on mobile devices and avoiding unnecessary network load.","<picture>  \\n  <source media=\\"(min-width: 800px)\\" srcset=\\"large.jpg\\">  \\n  <source media=\\"(min-width: 400px)\\" srcset=\\"medium.jpg\\">  \\n  <img src=\\"small.jpg\\" alt=\\"An example image\\"> \\n</picture>"],[16,"`<canvas>` is an HTML tag used to draw 2D and 3D graphics through JavaScript. By itself `<canvas>` contains no graphics; a JavaScript API is used to create them. The element is useful for animations, games and the dynamic rendering of images, but it requires programming for every detail.","`<canvas>` is an HTML5 tag that provides a space for drawing graphics with JavaScript. By itself `<canvas>` contains no content beyond the \\"canvas\\" you can draw on. JavaScript and its API are used for that, to render 2D or 3D graphics. With this API you can draw shapes, images and text, and even create animations.\\nAn example of use:\\n\\nThis code creates a `<canvas>` element 500x500 pixels in size and draws a blue square on it with JavaScript. One of the advantages of `<canvas>` is its flexibility, but it requires much more effort than other graphical solutions, since every element is drawn programmatically and cannot be interactive without additional code.\\n`<canvas>` is often used to create complex graphical interfaces such as games, data visualizations or interactive animations, where high performance and control over every pixel are required.","<canvas id=\\"myCanvas\\" width=\\"500\\" height=\\"500\\"></canvas> \\n\\n<script>  \\n  const canvas = document.getElementById(\'myCanvas\');  \\n  const ctx = canvas.getContext(\'2d\');  \\n  ctx.fillStyle = \'blue\';  \\n  ctx.fillRect(50, 50, 100, 100); // We draw a blue square \\n<\/script>"],[17,"SVG (Scalable Vector Graphics) is a vector graphics format that lets you display images that scale without any loss of quality. SVG is used to create logos, icons, diagrams and other graphical elements that stay sharp at any resolution. SVG elements can be animated and controlled through CSS and JavaScript.","SVG (Scalable Vector Graphics) is an XML format for creating two-dimensional vector graphics that is supported by every modern browser. Unlike raster graphics (PNG or JPEG, for example), SVG stores information about objects as geometric shapes (lines, circles, polygons), which lets images be scaled without any loss of quality. This makes SVG ideal for logos, icons and other graphical elements that have to look sharp on devices with different resolutions.\\nAn example of use:\\n\\nThis code creates a red circle with a black outline. SVG supports not only simple shapes but also text, complex paths and transformations. SVG elements can also be styled and animated with CSS and controlled through JavaScript.\\nSVG is used actively to create interactive elements on web pages, such as charts, diagrams or interactive maps. Unlike `<canvas>`, SVG is part of the DOM, so every element can be manipulated just like any other HTML element.","<svg width=\\"100\\" height=\\"100\\">  \\n  <circle cx=\\"50\\" cy=\\"50\\" r=\\"40\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\" fill=\\"red\\" /> \\n</svg>"],[18,"To create a table in HTML the tags `<table>`, `<tr>`, `<td>`, `<th>`, `<thead>`, `<tbody>` and `<tfoot>` are used. The `<table>` tag creates the table itself, `<tr>` a row, `<td>` a cell and `<th>` a header cell. Additional tags such as `<thead>`, `<tbody>` and `<tfoot>` let you divide the table into sections.","Tables in HTML are created with several key tags:\\n\\n- `<table>` — the main tag, which wraps the whole table.\\n\\n- `<tr>` (table row) — defines a row of the table.\\n\\n- `<td>` (table data) — defines a cell containing data.\\n\\n- `<th>` (table header) — used for header cells in rows or columns; it is normally displayed in bold.\\n\\nAn example of a simple table:\\n\\nThis code creates a table with two columns: \\"Name\\" and \\"Age\\". In every row of the table the data goes in `<td>` cells and the headings in `<th>`.\\nTo improve the table\'s structure you can also use additional tags:\\n\\n- `<thead>` — groups the rows containing the column headings.\\n\\n- `<tbody>` — groups the rows with the table\'s main content.\\n\\n- `<tfoot>` — groups the rows at the bottom of the table, normally for totals.\\n\\nAn example using all the tags:\\n\\nTables in HTML are used to present tabular data and should not be applied to align elements on a page. Using them properly helps structure data and makes it easier to take in.","<table>  \\n  <tr>    \\n    <th>Name</th>    \\n    <th>Age</th>  \\n  </tr>  \\n  <tr>    \\n    <td>Alice</td>    \\n    <td>25</td>  \\n  </tr>  \\n  <tr>    \\n    <td>Bob</td>    \\n    <td>30</td>  \\n  </tr> \\n</table>\\n\\n<table>  \\n  <thead>    \\n    <tr>      \\n      <th>Name</th>      \\n      <th>Age</th>    \\n    </tr>  \\n  </thead>  \\n  <tbody>    \\n    <tr>      \\n      <td>Alice</td>      \\n      <td>25</td>    \\n    </tr>    \\n    <tr>      \\n      <td>Bob</td>      \\n      <td>30</td>    \\n    </tr>  \\n  </tbody>  \\n  <tfoot>    \\n    <tr>      \\n      <td>Total</td>      \\n      <td>55</td>    \\n    </tr> \\n  </tfoot>\\n</table>"],[19,"The `<head>` tag is used to hold information about a web page that is not displayed on the screen directly. It includes meta tags, links to styles and scripts, the page\'s title (through the `<title>` tag) and other elements necessary for the site to work properly. Everything inside `<head>` is not displayed on the web page directly, but is used by the browser to process the page.","The `<head>` tag is one of the mandatory elements of an HTML document, and it contains information that is invisible to users but important for browsers and search engines. Inside `<head>` you can place meta tags such as `<meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">` for stating the encoding and `<meta name=\\"description\\" content=\\"A description of the page\\">` for SEO, links to style files (`<link>`) and scripts (`<script>`), and set the page\'s title through the `<title>` tag.\\nAn example of use:\\n\\nThis code includes metadata about the page and a style file, attaches external JavaScript and sets the page title \\"My page\\", which is shown on the browser tab. Without a properly configured `<head>` a page may display or work incorrectly, and it will also perform poorly in search engines.","<head>  \\n  <meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">  \\n  <meta name=\\"description\\" content=\\"A description of the page\\">  \\n  <title>My page</title>  \\n  <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\">  \\n  <script src=\\"script.js\\"><\/script> \\n</head>"],[20,"Block elements take up the whole width of the available space, starting on a new line (`<div>`, `<h1>`, `<p>`, for example), while inline elements take up only as much space as they need and sit on the same line as other elements (`<span>`, `<a>`, `<strong>`, for example). Block elements can contain both other block elements and inline ones, whereas inline elements normally contain only text or other inline elements.","Block and inline elements have different display properties, which determines how they behave on a page. Block elements always start on a new line and take up the whole width of their parent container, even when their content takes up less space. `<div>`, `<h1>` and `<p>`, for example, are block elements. They are often used to create a page\'s main blocks.\\nInline elements, by contrast, sit on the same line as other elements and take up only the space their content requires. Examples of inline elements are `<span>`, `<a>` and `<strong>`. They are often applied to style individual words or parts of text without disturbing the overall flow of the content.\\nFor example:\\n\\nHere `<p>` is a block element that creates a new paragraph, while `<strong>` is an inline element that emphasizes the word \\"important\\" without interrupting the flow of the text.\\nThe difference between these elements matters for building a page\'s layout properly and for using CSS to style them. Block elements, for example, are easy to control through width and height properties, whereas inline ones require special styles such as `display: block;` in order to change their behaviour.","<p>This is <strong>important</strong> text inside a paragraph.</p>"],[21,"The `<noscript>` tag displays content when the user has JavaScript disabled or their browser does not support scripts. This is useful for making a site accessible to all users, including those who cannot use JavaScript. Inside `<noscript>` you can place alternative versions of content or instructions for users.","The `<noscript>` tag is an HTML tag that lets developers display a message or alternative content for users who have JavaScript disabled. In modern web applications JavaScript is often used to change a page\'s content dynamically, to work with APIs and to perform other interactive tasks. Not every user has JavaScript enabled, however, or uses a browser that supports scripts. In such cases `<noscript>` helps you provide important messages or basic functionality.\\nAn example of use:\\n\\nThis code shows the user a message if their browser does not support JavaScript or it is disabled. The `<noscript>` tag is often used to display warnings, instructions or even simplified versions of content, so that the user can interact with the site even without script support.\\nUsing the `<noscript>` tag improves a web page\'s accessibility and shows consideration for users whose JavaScript capabilities are disabled for whatever reason.","<noscript>  \\n  <p>Your browser does not support JavaScript or it is disabled. Please enable JavaScript for the site to work fully.</p> \\n</noscript>"],[22,"Data attributes are custom attributes beginning with `data-` that let you store additional data on HTML elements. That data can be used by JavaScript to process the elements on a page dynamically. Data attributes are convenient for passing data without any need to change the HTML structure or use hidden fields.","Data attributes are a mechanism for storing custom data on HTML elements using attributes that begin with `data-` — `data-id`, `data-name` and so on. These attributes do not affect how the content is displayed, but they let you store information that can easily be read out with JavaScript and used to work with the page\'s elements.\\nAn example of use:\\n\\nIn this example the button carries two data attributes: `data-product-id` and `data-category`. JavaScript can reach these values like this:\\n\\nData attributes are convenient for passing information from HTML into JavaScript without any need for additional elements or more complicated markup. They are used actively in dynamic interfaces, for managing events and for storing unique data associated with an element.","<button data-product-id=\\"12345\\" data-category=\\"books\\">Buy the book</button>\\n\\nconst button = document.querySelector(\'button\'); \\nconst productId = button.getAttribute(\'data-product-id\'); \\nconsole.log(productId); // 12345"],[23,"For a link to open in a new tab you need to add the attribute `target=\\"_blank\\"` to the `<a>` tag. This is the standard way of telling the browser that the link should be opened in a new window or tab. For better security it is also recommended to add the attribute `rel=\\"noopener noreferrer\\"`.","By default links created with the `<a>` tag open in the same tab. You can change this behaviour, however, by adding the attribute `target=\\"_blank\\"`. This attribute tells the browser to open the link in a new tab or window.\\nFor example:\\n\\nWhen the user clicks this link, it opens in a new tab. Using `target=\\"_blank\\"` creates a potential security vulnerability, however, since the new tab can reach the `window.opener` object, which could in theory allow the original page to be manipulated. To prevent this, the attribute `rel=\\"noopener noreferrer\\"` is added:\\n\\nThis combination of attributes is the recommended practice for protecting sites and users.","<a href=\\"https://example.com\\" target=\\"_blank\\">Go to the site</a>\\n\\n<a href=\\"https://example.com\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener noreferrer\\">Go to the site</a>"],[24,"To send an email through HTML you can use a link whose `href` attribute contains the `mailto:` protocol. This opens the user\'s mail application with a ready-made draft message. In the link you can give the address, the subject and the body of the message.","HTML lets you create links for sending email using the `mailto:` protocol. When a user clicks such a link, their mail client (Outlook, Gmail or another) opens with the \\"To\\", \\"Subject\\" and even \\"Body\\" fields filled in beforehand (if you wish).\\nAn example of a basic link:\\n\\nWhen the user clicks this link, their mail client opens with the \\"To\\" field filled in with `example@example.com` automatically.\\nYou can also add a subject and a body:\\n\\nThis code will create a message with the subject \\"Hello\\" and the text \\"The body of the message\\". It is important to understand, however, that sending mail this way requires a mail client to be configured on the device. The method is not suitable for sending mail from the server, which normally requires server-side code or external services.","<a href=\\"mailto:example@example.com\\">Send a message</a>\\n\\n<a href=\\"mailto:example@example.com?subject=Hello&body=The body of the message\\">Send a message</a>"],[25,"The `alt` attribute on the `<img>` tag is used to provide a textual description of an image. This matters for users who cannot see the image — people with visual impairments, for example, or when the image fails to load. Search engines also use this text to index images, which improves SEO.","The `alt` attribute (alternative text) on the `<img>` tag is meant to describe an image\'s content. This is especially useful for people with visual impairments who use screen readers to understand what a picture shows. If the image cannot be loaded, the text from the `alt` attribute is displayed in its place, which helps users understand the context.\\nAn example of use:\\n\\nIn this example, if the image `logo.png` fails to load, users will see the text \\"The logo of the XYZ company\\". The `alt` attribute is also useful for SEO, since search engines analyse this text to determine what images contain, which can help the page be indexed.\\nUsing the `alt` attribute properly is an important aspect of web accessibility that provides a better experience for every user and increases a site\'s visibility in search engines.","<img src=\\"logo.png\\" alt=\\"The logo of the XYZ company\\">"],[26,"The async and defer attributes on the <script> tag are used to control how and when the browser loads and executes JavaScript. async loads the script asynchronously and executes it as soon as it has loaded, without waiting for the whole page to finish loading. defer also loads the script asynchronously but postpones its execution until the HTML document has loaded fully, which prevents the page\'s rendering from being blocked.","When adding scripts to a page it is important to consider how they may affect performance and the user experience. By default, when the browser meets a `<script>` tag it pauses the page\'s rendering in order to load and execute the script, which can slow down the display of the content.\\nThe `async` attribute lets the browser load the script asynchronously, which means that loading the script does not block the page\'s rendering. As soon as the script has loaded it is executed, even if the HTML document has not finished loading.\\nFor example:\\n\\nThe `defer` attribute also loads the script asynchronously, but it postpones execution until the entire HTML document has loaded. This guarantees that the script runs only after the page has loaded fully.\\nFor example:\\n\\nUsing these attributes lets you optimize loading time and improve the user experience, especially on pages with a lot of scripts or with heavy JavaScript frameworks.","<script src=\\"script.js\\" async><\/script>\\n\\n<script src=\\"script.js\\" defer><\/script>"],[27,"JavaScript can be attached to HTML in three main ways: inline, through an external file, and through tag attributes. Inline JavaScript is placed inside a `<script>` tag in the HTML document. An external JavaScript file is attached with the tag `<script src=\\"file.js\\">`. You can also use attributes such as `onclick` to handle events directly in the HTML.","There are several ways of attaching JavaScript to HTML, and each has its own characteristics and advantages:\\nInline JavaScript: this way means writing the JavaScript code directly inside the HTML document within a `<script>` tag. It is convenient for small scripts, since all the code is in one place.\\n\\nAn external JavaScript file: for more complex applications it is recommended to keep the JavaScript code in a separate file. This makes managing and reusing the code easier. To attach an external file, the `src` attribute on the `<script>` tag is used.\\n\\nEvent handlers in HTML: JavaScript can also be attached using attributes such as `onclick`, `onmouseover` and others. This lets JavaScript code be executed in response to events associated with HTML elements.\\n\\nEach of these ways has its advantages. Inline code is simple to use but is not suitable for large projects.\\nExternal files help you keep things tidy, and event handlers let you add interactivity to a page\'s elements quickly. The right choice of approach depends on the size of the project and the structure of your code.","<script>\\n  console.log(\\"Hello, World!\\");\\n<\/script>\\n\\n<script src=\\"script.js\\"><\/script>\\n\\n<button onclick=\\"alert(\'Hello!\')\\">Click me</button>"],[28,"Accessibility is the practice of building websites that every user can use, including people with disabilities. It involves improvements such as using proper headings, text for screen readers, colour contrast and keyboard navigation. The goal is to give every user access to the information and functionality, regardless of their abilities.","Web accessibility (or a11y) is a set of methods and practices that let users with disabilities interact with a website effectively. Those disabilities may include problems with vision or hearing, or motor or cognitive impairments. For a site to be accessible, developers have to consider several aspects:\\n\\n- Text alternatives for images: the `alt` attribute on images, for example, matters for screen-reader users.\\n\\n- Keyboard navigation: every element on the site must be reachable without a mouse.\\n\\n- A proper heading structure: this helps screen readers and users understand and navigate the page better.\\n\\n- Colour contrast: text and interface elements must be easy to tell apart for people with colour blindness or other visual impairments.\\n\\nAccessibility is also governed by a number of standards such as the WCAG (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines). Observing those standards makes a site more inclusive and improves every user\'s interaction with the content.",null],[29,"SEO (Search Engine Optimization) is a set of methods that help improve a site\'s position in search results. It is achieved through a proper site structure, the use of relevant keywords, optimizing images, increasing loading speed and ensuring accessibility. SEO helps attract more visitors by increasing visibility in search engines such as Google.","SEO (search engine optimization) is the process of optimizing a website to improve its visibility in the organic (unpaid) results of search engines. Search engines such as Google index pages and determine their relevance with complex algorithms, taking into account factors such as the quality of the content, the site\'s structure and the user experience.\\n\\nThe main SEO methods include:\\n\\n- Keywords: it is important to use the queries users actually make in headings, meta descriptions and the page\'s text.\\n\\n- Quality content: regularly adding useful, original content helps improve your ranking in search engines.\\n\\n- Internal and external optimization: internal links, a proper heading structure (`<h1>`, `<h2>`), meta tags and descriptions all help improve a site\'s visibility.\\n\\n- Optimizing images: using compressed images and the `alt` attribute helps both performance and SEO.\\n\\n- Improving page loading speed: fast page loads matter both for SEO and for the user experience.\\n\\nSEO helps attract more organic traffic and improves a site\'s visibility and its ranking in search engines.",null],[30,"HTML5 added many new semantic tags such as `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<article>` and `<section>`, which help improve the structure and readability of code. Tags for multimedia elements were also added, such as `<audio>` and `<video>`, and support for forms was improved with new attributes. These changes make HTML5 more powerful and more convenient for building modern websites.","HTML5 brought many improvements to the HTML language, including new semantic tags, multimedia elements and form attributes, which make web development more intuitive and efficient. Here are some of the most significant changes:\\nSemantic tags: HTML5 added tags that help describe a web page\'s structure better. For example:\\n\\n- `<header>`: used for the top part of a page or section.\\n\\n- `<footer>`: denotes the bottom part of a page or section.\\n\\n- `<article>`: for self-contained pieces of content such as articles or blog posts.\\n\\n- `<section>`: for logically related sections of a page.\\n\\n- `<nav>`: for navigation links. These tags improve accessibility, since they help both search engines and screen readers understand the page\'s structure better.\\n\\nMultimedia elements: HTML5 introduced the `<audio>` and `<video>` tags, which let you insert multimedia content into a page directly without third-party plugins.\\n\\nNew attributes for forms: HTML5 also improved forms by adding attributes such as `placeholder`, input types (`email`, `date`, `number`, for example) and the `<datalist>` element, which lets you create dropdown lists with suggested values.\\nThese innovations make HTML5 a more powerful tool for building modern websites, improving semantics and accessibility and making the integration of multimedia content easier.","<audio controls>    \\n  <source src=\\"audio.mp3\\" type=\\"audio/mpeg\\"> \\n</audio> \\n<video controls>    \\n  <source src=\\"video.mp4\\" type=\\"video/mp4\\"> \\n</video>"],[31,"`data-` attributes do not cause a re-render by themselves, but if they change and are tied to a component\'s state, that can lead to the component being updated in React. Excessive use or frequent changes to `data-` attributes can hurt performance, since they can cause unnecessary repaints.","`data-*` attributes in HTML let developers store custom data directly on elements. These attributes are often used in JavaScript to reach additional information associated with elements, which can be useful for changing the content or behaviour of web pages dynamically.\\nWhen the values of these attributes are changed in JavaScript, that can cause the associated elements to re-render, especially in frameworks such as React. Depending on the implementation, the changes may lead to the virtual DOM being recomputed and synchronized with the real DOM, which can affect performance. The more elements have to be re-rendered, the more resources it takes, and this can slow the application down.\\nTo optimize performance when using `data-*` attributes, the following is worth considering:\\n\\n- Minimize the number of changes: try to avoid changing attribute values often, especially in large lists of elements.\\n\\n- Use batching: combining several changes into a single update can reduce the number of re-renders.\\n\\n- Introduce memoization: in some cases memoization can be used to prevent unnecessary computation and renders.\\n\\nManaging the use of `data-*` attributes properly can therefore improve the performance of web applications considerably.",null],[32,"The Shadow DOM creates an isolated DOM tree for a web component that is hidden from the rest of the document.\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- It isolates styles and markup.\\n\\n- It makes reuse easier.\\n\\n- It encapsulates logic.","The Shadow DOM is a technology that lets you create a \\"shadow\\" DOM inside an element, isolating its content and styles from the main document. This lets developers create web components that can carry their own styles and elements without affecting other parts of the page. The Shadow DOM makes elements more independent and more reusable.\\nWhen you create a Shadow DOM for an element — with the `attachShadow()` method, for example — a new context is created that does not inherit styles and scripts from the outer document. Here is an example of code demonstrating the creation of a Shadow DOM:\\n\\nThe advantages of the Shadow DOM:\\n\\n- Style isolation: styles inside the Shadow DOM do not conflict with global styles, which makes it ideal for developing components.\\n\\n- Encapsulation: everything inside the Shadow DOM is hidden from the outer environment, which reduces the chance of conflicts and errors.\\n\\n- Reusability: components with a Shadow DOM can easily be reused in different parts of an application, ensuring consistency and making maintenance easier.\\n\\nThe Shadow DOM is therefore a powerful tool for developing web components that provides encapsulation and protection from style conflicts.","class MyElement extends HTMLElement {    \\n  constructor() {        \\n    super();        \\n    const shadow = this.attachShadow({ mode: \'open\' });        \\n    shadow.innerHTML = `            \\n      <style>                \\n        p {                    \\n          color: blue;                \\n        }            \\n      </style>            \\n      <p>Hello from Shadow DOM!</p>        \\n    `;    \\n  } \\n} \\n\\ncustomElements.define(\'my-element\', MyElement);"],[33,"Progressive Enhancement and Graceful Degradation are two approaches to web development aimed at building adaptable, accessible web applications.\\nProgressive Enhancement: start from a basic version and add improvements for modern browsers.\\nGraceful Degradation: start from the full version and adapt it for older browsers.","Progressive Enhancement and Graceful Degradation are the two main approaches to developing web applications, and they help ensure those applications are accessible and compatible with various devices and browsers.\\n\\n- Progressive Enhancement: this approach starts from basic functionality that is available on every device and browser. At the first stage a simple version of the application is built with HTML, which provides the main content and structure. Then styles are added with CSS and finally more complex features with JavaScript. This ensures accessibility for every user, regardless of their device or browser. For example, if you are building a form, it will work with basic HTML elements, while styles and validation with JavaScript are added for newer browsers.\\n\\n- Graceful Degradation: in this approach the full version of the application is built first using modern technologies such as CSS and JavaScript. The developer then works on making the application available to less advanced browsers or devices that may not support every feature. For example, if you are building an interactive element with JavaScript, you can provide a simple text alternative for users who cannot use JavaScript.\\n\\nHTML as the foundation:\\nHTML supports both approaches by providing the basic structure and content. For example, you can use HTML to create a form that will work on every device. Then you can add CSS to improve its appearance and JavaScript to add interactivity. This lets developers build accessible, adaptable web applications that interact well with users regardless of their technology.\\nThese approaches therefore help you create web applications that work well on every device and provide a better user experience.",null],[34,"Ways of optimizing the loading of images in HTML:\\n\\n- Compressing images\\n\\n- Responsive sizes\\n\\n- Lazy loading\\n\\nImage formats:\\n\\n- WebP: high compression and quality; it supports both raster and animated images.\\n\\n- AVIF: even more efficient compression and better quality than WebP.","Optimizing the loading of images is an important part of web development, since images can take a significant amount of time to load and have a strong effect on a page\'s performance. Here are some of the main methods of optimizing image loading in HTML:\\nCompressing images: use tools to compress images without any loss of quality. This lets you reduce the file size and speed loading up. Formats such as JPEG and PNG can be compressed without any significant degradation in quality.\\nResponsive image sizes: use the `srcset` and `sizes` attributes on the `<img>` tag to provide images of various sizes depending on the device. This lets smaller images be loaded on mobile devices and larger ones on desktops:\\n\\nLazy loading: use the `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute on the `<img>` tag to defer the loading of images that are outside the visible area of the screen. This helps save resources and speeds up the page\'s initial load:\\n\\nModern image formats: WebP and AVIF are modern formats that provide better compression than traditional formats such as JPEG and PNG.\\nWebP: an image format from Google that supports both lossy and lossless compression. It gives you a smaller file size at a comparable image quality.\\nAVIF: a new format based on the AV1 codec, which also offers excellent compression and support for HDR. It can produce even smaller files than WebP while preserving high quality.\\nHere is an example of using the WebP format in HTML:\\n\\nThese optimization methods and modern image formats help improve the performance of web applications, reducing loading time and improving the user experience.","<img src=\\"image-small.jpg\\" srcset=\\"image-medium.jpg 600w, image-large.jpg 1200w\\" sizes=\\"(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 50vw\\" alt=\\"Example image\\">\\n\\n<img src=\\"image.jpg\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"Example image\\">\\n\\n<picture>    \\n  <source srcset=\\"image.webp\\" type=\\"image/webp\\">    \\n  <img src=\\"image.jpg\\" alt=\\"Example image\\"> \\n</picture>"],[35,"ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications) is a set of attributes that helps developers improve the accessibility of complex web applications for people with disabilities. ARIA attributes can be added to HTML elements to describe their roles, states and properties for screen readers and other assistive technologies. For example, ARIA attributes can help establish that an element is a button or a heading, and provide information about its state (\\"expanded\\" or \\"collapsed\\", for example). Using ARIA properly makes content clearer and more accessible for every user.","ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications) is a specification developed to improve the accessibility of web applications, especially those that use dynamic interfaces and interactive elements such as buttons, tabs and dropdown lists. With ARIA attributes developers can provide additional information about the functionality and state of those elements, which makes it easier for users with disabilities to interact with web applications.\\nThe key aspects of using ARIA:\\nRoles (role): the `role` attribute defines an element\'s type and its function. For example, if you are building a custom element that should behave like a button, you can use `role=\\"button\\"`:\\n\\nStates (aria attributes): attributes such as `aria-expanded`, `aria-pressed` and `aria-hidden` help communicate the state of elements. For a dropdown menu, for example, you can use:\\n\\nValues (aria-label, aria-labelledby, aria-describedby): these attributes help provide an additional description of an element. For example:\\n\\nUse:\\nIt is important to remember that ARIA attributes cannot replace semantic HTML. They should be used in addition to standard HTML elements, not instead of them. For example, rather than using a `div` with `role=\\"button\\"`, it is better to use a `<button>` element, which is already semantically correct.\\nHere is an example of using ARIA properly in a form:\\n\\nApplying ARIA properly therefore helps make web applications more accessible, which matters for giving everyone equal access to online resources.","<div role=\\"button\\" tabindex=\\"0\\">Click me</div>\\n\\n<button aria-expanded=\\"false\\">Menu</button>\\n\\n<button aria-label=\\"Close the window\\">X</button>\\n\\n<form>    \\n  <label for=\\"username\\">User name:</label>    \\n  <input type=\\"text\\" id=\\"username\\" aria-required=\\"true\\" />    \\n  <button type=\\"submit\\" aria-label=\\"Submit the form\\">Submit</button> \\n</form>"],[36,"HTML5 provides various input formats such as `<input type=\\"email\\">`, `<input type=\\"url\\">` and `<input type=\\"number\\">`, which check the data entered against particular criteria automatically. Built-in validation happens automatically when a form is submitted, and the browser notifies the user of errors if the data does not match the format. Custom validation, by contrast, lets developers create their own rules for checking data with JavaScript, which gives more control and flexibility.","HTML5 introduced many new input types and improved the mechanism for validating form data. This lets developers build more interactive, more convenient web applications with less time spent writing custom code.\\nInput formats in HTML5:\\nSome of the new input types include:\\n\\n- `<input type=\\"email\\">`: checks that the value entered has the form of an email address (user@example.com, for example).\\n\\n- `<input type=\\"url\\">`: checks that the value entered matches the form of a URL (https://example.com, for example).\\n\\n- `<input type=\\"number\\">`: restricts the input to numbers only and can include attributes for setting a range (`min` and `max`, for example).\\n\\nBuilt-in validation:\\nHTML5 provides built-in form validation, which happens automatically on submission. If the data does not meet the requirements that have been set (an incorrect email format, for example), the browser shows an error message and prevents the form from being submitted. For example:\\n\\nIf the user enters an invalid email, the browser notifies them of it.\\nCustom validation:\\nCustom validation lets developers add their own rules for checking data. This is done with JavaScript and events such as `submit` or `input`. For example, you can create a check based on a particular condition:\\n\\nA comparison:\\n\\n- Built-in validation: an automatic check that the data matches the types you have set, with no need to write additional code.\\n\\n- Custom validation: lets you create more complex, more specific checks that the standard facilities cannot perform.\\n\\nBoth approaches can be used together to provide the best user experience and improve the quality of the data entered.","<form>    \\n  <label for=\\"email\\">Email:</label>    \\n  <input type=\\"email\\" id=\\"email\\" required>    \\n  <button type=\\"submit\\">Submit</button> \\n</form>\\n\\nconst form = document.querySelector(\'form\'); \\nform.addEventListener(\'submit\', function(event) {    \\n  const email = document.getElementById(\'email\').value;    \\n  if (!email.endsWith(\'@example.com\')) {        \\n    alert(\'The email must end with @example.com\');        \\n    event.preventDefault(); // prevents the form from being submitted    \\n  } \\n});"],[37,"Content Security Policy (CSP) is a security mechanism that helps prevent various kinds of attack, such as XSS (Cross-Site Scripting), by restricting which resources may be loaded on a page. The CSP header defines where scripts, styles and other resources are allowed to be loaded from.\\nThe `integrity` attribute on the `<script>` tag is used to check the integrity of the script being loaded, in order to make sure it has not been altered. If the script\'s hash does not match the given value, the browser refuses to load it.","Content Security Policy (CSP) is a powerful security tool that lets web developers control which resources may be loaded and executed on their pages. CSP helps prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks and other security threats by creating policies for loading resources.\\nAn example of using CSP:\\nCSP is configured through the HTTP header `Content-Security-Policy`. Here is an example:\\n\\nThis header tells the browser to allow resources to be loaded only from the same origin (`\'self\'`) or from a trusted CDN. This limits an attacker\'s ability to load a malicious script.\\nThe `integrity` attribute:\\nThe `integrity` attribute is used on `<script>` and `<link>` tags to check the integrity of the resource being loaded. It contains a hash of the file\'s value, which must match the hash of the resource that was loaded. For example:\\n\\nIf the browser cannot verify that the script it loaded matches the given hash, it will not load it. This prevents altered or malicious code from being injected.\\nCombining CSP and `integrity`:\\nUsing CSP together with the `integrity` attribute creates a powerful layer of protection for web applications. It lets you not only control where resources may be loaded from but also verify their integrity before execution. This matters especially in modern web applications, where third-party libraries and frameworks are often used.\\nUnderstanding and applying CSP and the `integrity` attribute properly therefore helps developers protect their applications from a great many common vulnerabilities.","Content-Security-Policy: default-src \'self\'; script-src \'self\' https://trusted.cdn.com;\\n\\n<script src=\\"https://trusted.cdn.com/script.js\\"        \\n  integrity=\\"sha384-oqVuAfXRKap7fdgcCY5Dn4Q3U8d6X7Ww1yq1GZ7E4j6Ic1ZrPy1X99AY2p3a8HnB\\"                 \\n  crossorigin=\\"anonymous\\">\\n<\/script>"],[38,"HTML pages can be protected with various measures, such as using security headers (Content Security Policy, for example), validating data properly and guarding against XSS attacks. These measures help restrict access to resources and prevent malicious code from being injected. It is also important to observe the principles of secure coding and to use HTTPS to protect users\' data. These approaches help minimize the security risks on a website.","The security of web pages is a critically important aspect of development that demands attention at every level. There are many methods that can help increase the security of HTML pages and protect them from threats such as XSS (Cross-Site Scripting), CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) and others.\\nThe key aspects of HTML page security:\\nContent Security Policy (CSP):\\nCSP is a mechanism that lets web developers control which resources may be loaded and executed on their pages. Using the CSP header, for example, you can allow scripts to be loaded only from particular origins:\\n\\nValidating data:\\nIt is important to check the data users enter in order to avoid malicious code being injected. The built-in form validation mechanisms in HTML5 and custom checks in JavaScript can help prevent XSS attacks:\\n\\nUsing HTTPS:\\nEncrypting data with HTTPS protects the information passed between client and server and prevents attackers from intercepting it.\\nSecurity headers:\\nBesides CSP there are other headers, such as `X-Content-Type-Options`, `X-Frame-Options` and `X-XSS-Protection`, that help protect pages from various attacks.\\nAuthentication and authorization:\\nProtecting pages from unauthorized access through authentication mechanisms such as OAuth, and through authorization, helps restrict access to important resources.\\nBy applying these measures developers can increase the security of HTML pages considerably and protect their users from potential threats.","Content-Security-Policy: default-src \'self\'; script-src \'self\' https://trusted.cdn.com;\\n\\nconst userInput = document.querySelector(\'#input\').value; \\nif (/<script>/.test(userInput)) {    \\n  alert(\'Invalid input!\'); \\n}"],[39,"`<template>` is a special HTML element that lets you create fragments of code that are not displayed on the page until they are activated with JavaScript. The `<slot>` element is used in custom elements to define a place where content passed to the element can be inserted. These elements help developers create more flexible, more reusable components that can change their content dynamically depending on the context.","HTML5 introduced the `<template>` and `<slot>` elements, which considerably simplify the creation of dynamic interfaces and custom elements. These tools help developers manage a page\'s content efficiently and create reusable components.\\nThe `<template>` element:\\nThis element is used to define a fragment of HTML that will not be displayed on the page until it is activated. It is useful for creating dynamic content without placing it in the DOM directly. For example:\\n\\nTo use this template you can get hold of it with JavaScript and clone it:\\n\\nThe `<slot>` element:\\nThis element is used inside custom elements to mark the places where the user\'s content can be inserted. It lets you create flexible components that can accept different kinds of content. An example of a custom element using a slot:\\n\\nIn this example the content with the attribute `slot=\\"header\\"` will be inserted into the heading, and the rest of the content into the main paragraph.\\nUse:\\nUsing `<template>` and `<slot>` lets developers create powerful, reusable components that can be configured dynamically depending on the context. This makes development more efficient and simplifies the management of content on pages.\\nThese tools therefore help you write cleaner, better organized and more efficient code, which makes them important for modern web applications.","<template id=\\"myTemplate\\">    \\n  <div class=\\"item\\">A list item</div> \\n</template>\\n\\nconst template = document.getElementById(\'myTemplate\').content; \\nconst clone = document.importNode(template, true); \\ndocument.body.appendChild(clone);\\n\\n<template id=\\"myComponent\\">    \\n  <div>        \\n    <h1><slot name=\\"header\\"></slot></h1>        \\n    <p><slot></slot></p>    \\n  </div> \\n</template> \\n\\n<my-component>    \\n  <span slot=\\"header\\">A heading</span>    \\n  This content will be inserted into the main slot. \\n</my-component>"],[40,"Meta tags are HTML elements that provide information about a page to the browser and to search engines. For example, `<meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">` sets the character encoding, which matters for text to display correctly. `<meta name=\\"viewport\\">` is used to control how the page is displayed on mobile devices, letting the content adapt to the screen\'s dimensions. Meta tags such as `<meta http-equiv>` can set parameters related to HTTP headers, which also helps optimize the page.","Meta tags play an important part in the optimization and correct display of web pages. They live in the `<head>` section of an HTML document and provide information that browsers, search engines and other clients can use.\\nThe character encoding:\\nUsing `<meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">` sets the document\'s character encoding. UTF-8 is the most widespread encoding and supports a great many languages and characters, which ensures that text displays correctly.\\n\\nSettings for mobile devices:\\nThe `<meta name=\\"viewport\\">` tag controls the page\'s dimensions and scale on mobile devices. This matters especially for responsive design. For example:\\n`<meta name=\\"viewport\\" content=\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\\">`\\nThis tells the browser that the page\'s width should match the device\'s width, and sets the initial scale.\\nHTTP headers:\\nMeta tags such as `<meta http-equiv=\\"X-UA-Compatible\\" content=\\"IE=edge\\">` can tell browsers how to process a page. This tag helps ensure compatibility with the latest HTML and CSS standards:\\n\\nSEO and social media:\\nMeta tags are also used for SEO and social media. For example, `<meta name=\\"description\\" content=\\"A description of the page\\">` gives search engines a brief description of the page. Open Graph tags (`<meta property=\\"og:title\\">`, `<meta property=\\"og:description\\">`) help optimize the content when links are posted on social platforms.\\nUsing the right meta tags not only improves a page\'s performance and accessibility but also helps SEO, which can increase a site\'s visibility in search engines. This makes them an important part of developing web pages, especially given modern standards and requirements.","<meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">\\n\\n<meta http-equiv=\\"X-UA-Compatible\\" content=\\"IE=edge\\">"],[41,"Microdata is a format built into HTML that helps structure the data on a page so that search engines and other services can recognize and interpret it easily. Using attributes such as `itemprop` and `itemscope`, developers can describe entities — products or articles, for example. Unlike JSON-LD, which is stored separately from the main HTML code, microdata is added directly into the page\'s markup, which can be simpler to integrate but less flexible to change. RDFa is another alternative that can be integrated with any HTML element.","Microdata is a technology built into HTML5 that is used to structure the data on a web page so that search engines and applications can understand its content better. It helps you describe entities such as people, products, events and places, and tie them to particular attributes and values that search engines can index.\\nAn example of using microdata:\\nImagine a web page that contains information about a book. With microdata we can describe the book\'s main properties:\\n\\nHere `itemscope` indicates that the element contains data about a book, while `itemprop` defines properties such as the name, the author and the publication date.\\nThe advantages of microdata over JSON-LD and RDFa:\\nMicrodata:\\nIt is embedded directly in the HTML, which makes it easier to use in existing markup. This can be useful if a developer wants to integrate semantic data without adding a separate block of code, as JSON-LD requires.\\nJSON-LD:\\nJSON-LD is stored separately from the HTML and delivered as a script. It is more flexible for working with large volumes of structured data and easier to generate and change automatically.\\nRDFa:\\nThis is another way of adding semantic markup, and it integrates with any HTML element. RDFa can be more universal, since it does not depend on specific attributes the way microdata does.\\nMicrodata is useful for embedding semantic data directly in HTML, but JSON-LD is considered more flexible and is supported by search engines such as Google. The choice between these methods depends on the complexity of the data and on the project\'s requirements.","<div itemscope itemtype=\\"http://schema.org/Book\\">   \\n  <h2 itemprop=\\"name\\">The book\'s title</h2>   \\n  <span itemprop=\\"author\\">The book\'s author</span>   \\n  <span itemprop=\\"datePublished\\">2024</span> \\n</div>"],[42,"The HTML Custom Elements API lets developers create their own HTML elements that behave like standard elements but can carry their own unique logic and styling. To create a custom element you have to define a class that inherits from `HTMLElement` and register it with `customElements.define()`. These elements can then be used on a page just like standard HTML tags. Custom Elements extend HTML\'s capabilities, making it more flexible for modern web applications.","The HTML Custom Elements API is part of the Web Components specification, and it lets developers create their own HTML elements with custom logic and behaviour. Custom Elements let you define new kinds of element that can be reused anywhere in an application, improving the structure and modularity of the code.\\nCreating a custom element:\\nTo create a custom element you have to take three steps:\\n\\n- Create a class inheriting from `HTMLElement`:\\n\\n- Register the element with the browser using `customElements.define()`:\\n\\n- Use the custom element on the page:\\n\\nOnce registered, the `my-custom-element` element can be used anywhere in an HTML page, like an ordinary tag.\\nThe advantages of custom elements:\\n\\n- Encapsulated logic: the element\'s behavioural logic is isolated entirely inside the class, which makes the code better organized and more modular.\\n\\n- Reuse: once you have created a custom element, it can be used repeatedly in different parts of the application, which reduces code duplication.\\n\\nCustom Elements are a tool for creating reusable components in web applications. They make HTML more flexible by letting developers define new elements with unique functions and behaviour.","class MyCustomElement extends HTMLElement {    \\n  constructor() {        \\n    super();        \\n    this.innerHTML = \\"<p>This is a custom element</p>\\";    \\n  } \\n}\\n\\ncustomElements.define(\'my-custom-element\', MyCustomElement);\\n\\n<my-custom-element></my-custom-element>"],[43,"The `tabindex` attribute controls the order in which the elements on a page receive focus during keyboard navigation. Elements with a positive `tabindex` receive focus in ascending order of that number, elements with `tabindex=\\"0\\"` in the order they appear, and negative values make an element unreachable by keyboard focus. The `focus()` method in JavaScript lets you set focus on an element programmatically. This matters for ensuring accessibility and convenient control for users with special needs.","Focus on a web page is the mechanism that determines which element is active and ready for the user to interact with. It matters for navigating a page by keyboard and for making web content accessible to people with disabilities.\\nThe `tabindex` attribute:\\nThe `tabindex` attribute controls the order in which the elements on a page will receive focus when the `Tab` key is used. The values of `tabindex` can be:\\n\\n- Positive values: elements with a positive `tabindex` receive focus first, in ascending order of the value. For example, an element with `tabindex=\\"1\\"` will receive focus before one with `tabindex=\\"2\\"`.\\n\\n- The value `0`: elements with `tabindex=\\"0\\"` take part in the standard focus navigation but have no priority.\\n\\n- Negative values: elements with a negative `tabindex`, such as `tabindex=\\"-1\\"`, cannot be focused by keyboard, but focus can be set on them programmatically with JavaScript.\\n\\nAn example of using `tabindex`:\\n\\nIn this example the focus order will be: Button 1, Button 2, Button 3, since `tabindex` determines the sequence.\\nThe `focus()` method:\\nIn JavaScript the `focus()` method lets you set focus on an element programmatically. This is useful for managing focus when the interface changes, or when you need to draw the user\'s attention to a particular element:\\n\\nAn example of using `focus()` to improve accessibility: when the user closes a modal window, focus can be returned to the element it was opened from:\\n\\nManaging focus with `tabindex` and methods such as `focus()` matters for ensuring accessibility and improving the user experience. This is especially critical for users who rely on the keyboard to navigate pages, such as people with disabilities.","<button tabindex=\\"1\\">Button 1</button> \\n<button tabindex=\\"3\\">Button 3</button> \\n<button tabindex=\\"2\\">Button 2</button>\\n\\ndocument.getElementById(\'myButton\').focus();\\n\\nbutton.addEventListener(\'click\', function() {\\n  modal.close();    \\n  button.focus(); // We return focus to the button after closing \\n});"],[44,"HTML5 offers new tags and features that older browsers do not always support. So that pages work properly in such browsers, fallback mechanisms are used — alternative content or tags that are picked up when the main functionality is unavailable. For video, for example, you can use an `<object>` element or a link to download the file if `<video>` is not supported. For cross-browser compatibility, polyfills and shims are also applied — scripts that add support for modern features to older browsers.","Fallback mechanisms and cross-browser compatibility are important aspects of development that keep a web page\'s functionality available in different browsers, including older versions.\\nAn example of a fallback for `<video>`:\\n\\nIn this example, if the browser does not support `<video>`, the user will see the text and a link for downloading the video.\\nA fallback for CSS:\\nIf a CSS property is not supported, you can use obsolete or alternative styles:\\n\\nCross-browser compatibility:\\nTo achieve compatibility the following are often applied:\\n\\n- Polyfills: these are scripts that add support for modern features to older browsers. A polyfill for `fetch()` or `Promise`, for example.\\n\\n- Shims: similar to polyfills, but they replace unavailable functions with alternatives.\\n\\nAn example of a polyfill for `fetch()`: if the browser does not support `fetch()`, you can use a polyfill:\\n\\nCross-browser compatibility techniques:\\n\\n- Progressive Enhancement: basic functionality for everyone, enhanced functionality for newer browsers.\\n\\n- Graceful Degradation: features for modern browsers are developed first, and then fallback mechanisms are added for older ones.\\n\\n- Using feature detection: instead of checking the browser\'s version, specific capabilities are checked:\\n\\nUsing fallback mechanisms and polyfills matters for keeping web pages working on devices with differing support for modern standards. It helps make a site available to a greater number of users.","<video controls>  \\n  <source src=\\"video.mp4\\" type=\\"video/mp4\\">  \\n  <source src=\\"video.ogv\\" type=\\"video/ogg\\">  Your browser does not support the video tag.  \\n  <a href=\\"video.mp4\\">Download the video</a> \\n</video>\\n\\n.example {  \\n  background: rgb(255, 0, 0); /* the old syntax */  \\n  background: linear-gradient(red, yellow); /* the new syntax */ \\n}\\n\\n<script src=\\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/fetch/2.0.4/fetch.min.js\\"><\/script>\\n\\nif (\'querySelector\' in document) {    \\n  // code for modern browsers \\n}"],[45,"CORS is a mechanism that lets you restrict or permit the exchange of data between different domains. Browsers forbid requests to resources on another domain by default, for security. To permit such requests, the server has to send special headers. Elements such as `<iframe>`, `<img>` and `<script>` can load resources from other domains, but some operations — reaching the content of an `<iframe>` from another domain, for example — require CORS support.","CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a web browser security policy that prevents cross-domain HTTP requests from being performed unless the server has permitted them explicitly. It exists to protect web applications from attacks such as CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) and XSS (Cross-Site Scripting).\\nHow CORS works:\\nWhen the browser tries to fetch a resource from another domain, it sends a preflight request with the `OPTIONS` method in order to find out whether the server permits access. If the server does permit requests from another domain, it sends a response with the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header naming the permitted domain (or the `*` character, which means any domain).\\nAn example of CORS headers:\\n`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://example.com Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST`\\nThese headers let the browser understand that requests from the domain `https://example.com` may be performed.\\nCORS and embedded elements:\\n\\n- `<iframe>`: without CORS support, access to the content of an iframe from another domain will be blocked. This protects against manipulation of content loaded from third-party sources.\\n\\n- `<img>` and `<script>`: these tags can load resources from other domains without restriction, but access to the loaded data (the pixels of an image, for example) will be blocked if the server does not support CORS.\\n\\nAn example of using CORS with `fetch()`:\\n\\nCORS matters for the security of web applications, letting you control which domains may reach your resources. Developers need to configure the headers on the server correctly in order to permit or restrict access from other domains.","fetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\', {  \\n  method: \'GET\',  \\n  headers: {    \\n    \'Origin\': \'https://mywebsite.com\'  \\n  } \\n}) \\n.then(response => response.json()) \\n.then(data => console.log(data)) \\n.catch(error => console.error(\'Error:\', error));"],[46,"The `srcset` attribute on the `<img>` tag lets you specify several versions of an image with different resolutions or sizes. The browser chooses the most suitable version automatically depending on the screen\'s pixel density and width. Combined with the `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute you can use lazy loading of images to optimize the page\'s performance.","The `srcset` attribute is used with `<img>` to give several versions of the same image, so that the browser can choose the most suitable one for the current device. The format of use may involve different image sizes (`image-1x.jpg 1x, image-2x.jpg 2x`, for example) or different widths (`image-small.jpg 500w, image-large.jpg 1000w`).\\nDepending on the device, the browser loads the image with the resolution or size it needs, ensuring optimal performance.\\nThe `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute lets the browser load images only when they come into the user\'s view. This reduces the page\'s loading time and the amount of data downloaded.\\nAn example of use:\\n\\nIn this example the browser will choose a suitable image depending on the screen\'s width and load it when it becomes visible.","<img src=\\"image-default.jpg\\" \\n    srcset=\\"image-500w.jpg 500w, image-1000w.jpg 1000w\\" \\n    sizes=\\"(max-width: 600px) 480px, 800px\\"\\n    loading=\\"lazy\\" \\n    alt=\\"A description of the image\\">"],[47,"Lazy loading is a technique that lets you defer the loading of images or frames until they appear in the visible area of the screen. The `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute on an `<img>` or `<iframe>` tag defers the element\'s loading automatically. This reduces the page\'s initial loading time and lowers data usage. Alternative methods include using the Intersection Observer API for more flexible control over when elements load.","Lazy loading is a performance optimization method that lets resources be loaded only when the user needs them — that is, when they come into the visible area of the screen (the viewport). It is useful for pages with a lot of images or video, where loading every resource at once can slow the page\'s initial load down.\\nThe `loading` attribute:\\nHTML5 added native support for lazy loading on `<img>` and `<iframe>` elements through the `loading` attribute.\\n\\nWhen this attribute is used, the browser loads the image only when it is going to be in the visible area of the screen.\\nThe advantages of `loading=\\"lazy\\"`:\\n\\n- Simplicity of use: adding the attribute requires minimal changes to the code.\\n\\n- Better performance: it speeds up the page\'s rendering, since it reduces the number of resources loaded.\\n\\n- Browser support: modern browsers support this attribute, which makes it available without third-party libraries.\\n\\nAlternative methods:\\nThe Intersection Observer API: this API gives you more control over when elements should load. It tracks when an element comes into the visible area and triggers the loading of the resource.\\n\\nDeferred loading with JavaScript: you can defer loading by hand, changing the value of the `src` attribute on images or `<iframe>` elements only when they become visible.\\nLazy loading helps reduce a page\'s loading time and save the user\'s data. Although the `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute is a convenient, simple solution, more complex scenarios can be implemented with the Intersection Observer for fine-tuning the loading process.","<img src=\\"image.jpg\\" alt=\\"Lazy loaded image\\" loading=\\"lazy\\">\\n\\nconst img = document.querySelector(\'img\'); \\nconst observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries, observer) => {  \\n  entries.forEach(entry => {    \\n    if (entry.isIntersecting) {      \\n      entry.target.src = entry.target.dataset.src;      \\n      observer.unobserve(entry.target);    \\n    }  \\n  }); \\n}); \\nobserver.observe(img);"],[48,"The `<dialog>` element lets you create built-in modal windows on a web page without any need for third-party libraries. It has the methods `.showModal()` for displaying the window in modal mode and `.close()` for closing it. One of its particulars is built-in support for modality and for trapping focus on the elements inside the dialog, which makes it convenient for creating interactive pop-up windows. It also supports attributes for configuring its appearance and behaviour.","The `<dialog>` element appeared in HTML5 and is intended for creating modal or non-modal dialogs such as pop-up windows. Its key task is to display a window on top of the main content while managing the user\'s focus.\\nThe element\'s main methods and attributes:\\nThe `.showModal()` method: opens the window in modal mode, blocking interaction with the rest of the page\'s elements until it is closed.\\n\\nThe window becomes modal, and the user cannot interact with elements outside the dialog.\\nThe `.close()` method: closes the dialog window.\\n\\nThe `open` attribute: if present, it makes the dialog visible without modality (non-modal mode).\\n\\nThe particulars of using it:\\n\\n- Focus: when the dialog is open in modal mode, focus stays inside it, which improves accessibility (a11y) and makes interaction with the interface more predictable for users.\\n\\n- Closing by default: if the user needs to close the dialog by pressing \\"Esc\\" or by clicking outside the window, that behaviour can be handled through events.\\n\\nExamples of use:\\n\\nThe `<dialog>` element lets you simplify the development of modal windows and improve their accessibility, and it supports modal behaviour without any complex libraries.","<dialog id=\\"myDialog\\">This is a modal dialog</dialog> \\n\\n<script> document.getElementById(\'myDialog\').showModal();<\/script>\\n\\ndocument.getElementById(\'myDialog\').close();\\n\\n<dialog open>This is an open dialog</dialog>\\n\\n<dialog id=\\"myDialog\\">  \\n  <p>Example of dialog content.</p>  \\n  <button onclick=\\"this.closest(\'dialog\').close()\\">Close</button> \\n</dialog> \\n<button onclick=\\"document.getElementById(\'myDialog\').showModal()\\">Open Dialog</button>"],[49,"HTML Imports were an experimental technology that let you load and include HTML files in other documents using the `<link>` tag. They were rejected because of weak support and the existence of alternative standards such as JavaScript modules and Web Components. Modern alternatives include ES modules (import/export) and technologies based on templates and components, such as React and Web Components.","HTML Imports were proposed as a way of including HTML code in other documents modularly. With the `<link>` tag you could load an external HTML file and include its content in a page:\\n\\nThis technology never gained wide acceptance, however, and was rejected for a number of reasons:\\n\\n- Limited browser support: HTML Imports were supported only in some browsers, such as Chrome, which made them unsuitable for cross-browser projects.\\n\\n- Performance difficulties: importing HTML files could increase loading time and make dependency management harder.\\n\\n- Better alternatives: the ECMAScript standard (ES6) offered more universal modules based on JavaScript, which handle the task of splitting and reusing code better.\\n\\nModern alternatives:\\n\\n- ES modules (JavaScript): this is the standardized way of organizing and loading code. With the `import`/`export` directives you can split code into modules that are easy to connect to one another.\\n\\n- Web Components: components based on the Web Components standard let you create custom elements containing HTML, CSS and JavaScript, which makes them an excellent alternative for building a modular interface.\\n\\nHTML Imports therefore failed to gain support because of the appearance of more capable standards such as ES modules and Web Components, which provide flexibility and power for building modular applications.","<link rel=\\"import\\" href=\\"header.html\\">\\n\\n// module.js \\nexport const myFunction = () => { console.log(\\"Hello, World!\\"); }; \\n// main.js \\nimport { myFunction } from \'./module.js\'; \\nmyFunction();\\n\\nclass MyComponent extends HTMLElement {  \\n  connectedCallback() {    \\n    this.innerHTML = `<p>Custom component content</p>`;  \\n  } \\n} \\ncustomElements.define(\'my-component\', MyComponent);"],[50,"Optimizing rendering involves minimizing reflow operations (rebuilding the document\'s structure) and repaints (updating visual changes). This is achieved by reducing how often the DOM changes, by buffering changes through `requestAnimationFrame` and by using CSS transforms, which do not cause a reflow. It is also important to reduce the number of heavy operations such as changing the size and position of elements.","The process of rendering HTML involves several stages: building the DOM tree and the render tree, computing styles and applying them, and the reflow and repaint processes.\\n\\n- Reflow: this is the process in which the browser recalculates the position and size of every element on the page when the DOM or the CSS changes. It is one of the most expensive operations.\\n\\n- Repaint: this is the process of updating the visible part of the page without changing its structure (changing a background colour, for example).\\n\\nFrequent reflows and repaints can slow a page down considerably, especially on mobile devices with low performance.\\nOptimization methods:\\n\\n- Minimizing changes to the DOM: changing a large number of DOM elements one at a time causes a reflow and a repaint for every change. It is better to group the changes or to use DOM fragments to insert several elements at once.\\n\\n- Using CSS transforms: changes through `transform` and `opacity` normally do not cause a reflow, since they do not require the position of elements to be recalculated.\\n\\n- Buffering changes with `requestAnimationFrame`: this lets the browser perform the updates before the screen\'s next repaint, preventing frequent repaints.\\n\\n- Avoiding the reading of properties that cause a reflow: properties such as `offsetHeight` or `clientWidth` cause a reflow, since the browser has to recalculate their values. Try to cache the results instead, or read such properties less often.\\n\\nMinimizing reflows and repaints can therefore increase a page\'s performance considerably, especially when working with dynamic content and animations.","const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment(); \\nfor (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {  \\n  const div = document.createElement(\'div\');  \\n  fragment.appendChild(div); \\n} \\ndocument.body.appendChild(fragment);\\n\\n.animate {  transform: translateX(100px); /* A fast animation without a reflow */ }\\n\\nlet element = document.getElementById(\'myElement\'); \\nrequestAnimationFrame(() => {  element.style.left = \'100px\'; });"],[51,"For multilingual support in HTML the `lang` and `dir` attributes are used. The `lang` attribute states the language of the content (`lang=\\"en\\"` for English, for example), while `dir` states the direction of the text (`dir=\\"rtl\\"` for languages read from right to left, for example). To manage dynamic localization, JavaScript and libraries such as i18next are often used, which let you change the interface\'s language according to the user\'s preferences.","Multilingual support (or internationalization, i18n) lets you display a web page\'s content in several languages, in order to make the site available to users from different countries. The main techniques involve using HTML attributes and third-party tools for changing the language dynamically.\\nThe `lang` attribute: this attribute is used to state the language of an element\'s content or of the whole page. It helps search engines, and screen readers too, interpret the content correctly.\\n\\nThe `dir` attribute: sets the direction of the text. For Arabic or Hebrew, for example, `dir=\\"rtl\\"` (right-to-left) is used, which changes the direction of the text on the page.\\n\\nLocalization with JavaScript: to change the language dynamically according to the user\'s choice or the browser\'s settings, you can use libraries such as i18next. These libraries let you keep translations in JSON files and substitute the appropriate values into the page.\\n\\nFor more complex applications dynamic localization is often applied, where the language and text direction can change in real time based on the user\'s settings.","<html lang=\\"en\\">  \\n  <body>    \\n    <p lang=\\"fr\\">Bonjour!</p> <!-- This paragraph is in French -->  \\n  </body> \\n</html>\\n\\n<p dir=\\"rtl\\">هذا نص عربي</p> <!-- Text in Arabic -->\\n\\nimport i18next from \'i18next\'; \\ni18next.init({  \\n  resources: {    \\n    en: { translation: { \\"welcome\\": \\"Welcome\\" } },    \\n    fr: { translation: { \\"welcome\\": \\"Bienvenue\\" } }  \\n  },  \\n  lng: \\"en\\", // The default language \\n}); \\ndocument.getElementById(\'welcome\').innerText = i18next.t(\'welcome\');"],[52,"The `<canvas>` element is used to draw graphics with JavaScript on a web page. With it you can create both simple shapes and complex animations and games. It has no built-in content, and every graphical element is drawn dynamically through methods such as `fillRect()` for drawing rectangles and `beginPath()` for creating lines and curves.","The `<canvas>` element is an HTML5 element that provides a place for drawing with JavaScript. Unlike other HTML elements, `<canvas>` does not display content directly but is a container in which graphics can be drawn with scripts. The `CanvasRenderingContext2D` API is used to work with it, and it provides methods for drawing shapes, lines, text and images.\\nAn example of simple use of `<canvas>`:\\n\\nWith the context\'s methods you can draw both simple shapes and more complex elements:\\n\\n- Rectangles: `fillRect(x, y, width, height)` draws a filled rectangle.\\n\\n- Lines and curves: `beginPath()` is used to create a path, after which lines can be added through `moveTo()` and `lineTo()`.\\n\\nAn example of drawing a circle:\\n\\nScenarios of use:\\n\\n- Games and animations: `<canvas>` is often used to create games, since it lets graphics be updated quickly and supports dynamic interaction.\\n\\n- Data visualization: it is also applied to create charts and diagrams.\\n\\n- Image editing: with `<canvas>` you can manipulate images (cropping, filters and so on).\\n\\nThe `<canvas>` element is a powerful tool for creating interactive graphics in the browser, providing a broad set of methods for working with 2D content.","<canvas id=\\"myCanvas\\" width=\\"200\\" height=\\"200\\"></canvas> \\n<script>  \\n  const canvas = document.getElementById(\'myCanvas\');  \\n  const ctx = canvas.getContext(\'2d\');  // We draw a rectangle  \\n  ctx.fillStyle = \'green\';  \\n  ctx.fillRect(10, 10, 150, 100); \\n<\/script>\\n\\nctx.beginPath(); \\nctx.arc(100, 100, 50, 0, Math.PI * 2); \\nctx.stroke();"],[53,"The HTML5 History API lets you change the URL in the browser without reloading the page, while preserving the navigation history. The API\'s main methods are `pushState()` and `replaceState()`, plus the `popstate` event. They let you change the page\'s state dynamically while updating the URL, which makes navigation in single-page applications (SPAs) smoother and more interactive.","The HTML5 History API is a set of methods for managing the browser\'s navigation history without reloading the page. In traditional web applications every link you follow leads to a new page being loaded. With the History API you can change a page\'s content and URL dynamically without reloading it fully, which makes the interaction smoother, especially in single-page applications (SPAs).\\nThe History API\'s main methods:\\n\\n- `history.pushState(state, title, url)`: adds a new entry to the browser\'s history with an updated URL. This method does not reload the page but lets you change the address bar.\\n\\n- `history.replaceState(state, title, url)`: replaces the current history entry without adding a new one, which is useful when you need to change the URL but not record a new step in the history.\\n\\n- `window.onpopstate`: this event fires when the user moves back or forward through the history. It can be used to update the page\'s content on such navigations.\\n\\nThe History API is used actively in modern frameworks such as React and Vue to manage routing in SPAs. This lets you make navigation more interactive and speed up the application\'s response.","history.pushState({page: 1}, \\"Title\\", \\"/new-url\\");\\n\\nhistory.replaceState({page: 2}, \\"Title\\", \\"/another-url\\");\\n\\nwindow.onpopstate = function(event) {  \\n  console.log(\\"location: \\" + document.location + \\", state: \\" + JSON.stringify(event.state)); \\n};"],[54,"A Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a network of servers located all over the world that hold copies of a website\'s static resources, such as images, CSS and JavaScript. Attaching resources through a CDN lets them be loaded from the nearest server, which speeds up the page\'s loading time and reduces the load on the main server. To attach them through a CDN, the URLs of the external resources are normally used, for example:\\n\\n`<script src=\\"https://cdn.example.com/library.js\\"><\/script>`","A Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a distributed network of servers used to store and deliver content such as images, styles, scripts and video to users based on their geographical location. A CDN\'s main purpose is to improve a site\'s performance by reducing loading time, since the resources are loaded from the server nearest the user.\\nThe advantages of using a CDN:\\n\\n- Faster page loads: since a CDN keeps copies of the files on servers all over the world, users can load the resources from the server closest to them, which reduces latency.\\n\\n- Less load on the main server: by distributing the traffic across many servers, the load on the origin server is reduced, which lets it handle more requests.\\n\\n- Reliability: if one server in the CDN fails, requests are redirected to the other available servers, which increases the site\'s resilience.\\n\\nAn example of attaching a resource through a CDN:\\n\\nIn this example the jQuery library is loaded from a CDN server, which improves the response time for users all over the world. Many popular libraries and frameworks, such as Bootstrap and React, are also served through CDNs, which makes attaching them easier.\\nUsing a CDN properly:\\n\\n- Caching: a CDN supports the caching of resources, which lets users load them faster on repeat visits to the site.\\n\\n- A fallback: it is also important to provide a fallback in case the CDN is temporarily unavailable. For example, you can give a local path to the file if the load from the CDN fails:\\n\\nUsing a CDN improves the performance and reliability of sites considerably, especially when serving a global audience.","<!-- Attaching the jQuery library through a CDN --> \\n<script src=\\"https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js\\"><\/script>\\n\\n<script src=\\"https://cdn.example.com/library.js\\"><\/script> <script>\\n  if(!window.library){\\n    document.write(\'<script src=\\"/local/library.js\\"><\\\\/script>\');\\n  }\\n<\/script>"],[55,"Web fonts such as Google Fonts can be attached through a `<link>` element in the `<head>` section of your HTML, or you can use the CSS `@import`. Fonts can slow a page\'s rendering down, however, so it is important to optimize their loading. The main factors affecting performance include the number of font styles, their format and the loading strategy (`font-display: swap`, for example, to avoid blocking the rendering of text).","Integrating web fonts (Google Fonts, for example) is a simple process that lets you use external fonts on your site. It is done through HTML and CSS. One of the most popular ways of attaching web fonts is using Google Fonts through a `<link>` tag in the HTML or the `@import` directive in CSS.\\nAn example of integration through HTML:\\n\\nAn example through CSS:\\n\\nWhen using web fonts it is important to consider performance, since the browser has to load the fonts before it can start displaying the text. Here are several factors that affect loading speed:\\n\\n- The number of fonts and their styles: the more fonts and variations (weights or italics, for example), the more data has to be loaded.\\n\\n- The font format: support for various formats (WOFF, WOFF2, TTF) matters for cross-browser compatibility. WOFF2 is the more compressed, preferable format.\\n\\n- Loading strategies: using the `font-display: swap` property lets the browser display the text with a fallback font first and then replace it with the web font once it has loaded. This prevents empty space on the page while the font is loading.\\n\\nAn example of optimizing with `font-display`:\\n\\nIntegrating and optimizing fonts properly improves the user experience by speeding up page loads and preventing delays in the display of text.","<head>\\n  <link\\n    href=\\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Roboto:wght@400;700&display=swap\\"\\n    rel=\\"stylesheet\\"\\n  />\\n</head>\\n\\n@import url(\\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Roboto:wght@400;700&display=swap\\");\\n\\n@font-face {\\n  font-family: \\"Roboto\\";\\n  src: url(\\"roboto.woff2\\") format(\\"woff2\\");\\n  font-display: swap;\\n}"],[56,"A webmanifest is a file in JSON format used to define a web application\'s metadata. It lets you configure the application\'s icons, name, theme and behaviour when it is added to a device\'s home screen. In a PWA the webmanifest helps make the web application resemble a native mobile app, improving the user experience.","A webmanifest is a file used in Progressive Web Apps (PWAs) to describe an application\'s metadata. This file tells the browser and the device important information such as the application\'s name, its icons, its colour scheme and its display parameters when the application is installed on the user\'s device.\\nThe manifest file is in JSON format and contains key fields such as:\\n\\n- `name`: the application\'s full name.\\n\\n- `short_name`: a short name, used on installation.\\n\\n- `icons`: a list of icons in different resolutions for display on the screen.\\n\\n- `start_url`: the URL the application starts from when it is opened.\\n\\n- `display`: determines how the application will look (`standalone`, for example, to launch in full screen without the browser interface).\\n\\n- `background_color` and `theme_color`: the background and theme colours used when the application is installed.\\n\\nAn example of a simple manifest:\\n\\nThis manifest lets users add the web application to their device\'s home screen, where it will behave like a native application, launching in its own window and with its own icons.\\nIn the context of a PWA, the webmanifest is an important component that makes a web application available offline and lets users interact with it as though it were a native mobile app.","{  \\n  \\"name\\": \\"My Awesome App\\",  \\n  \\"short_name\\": \\"AwesomeApp\\",  \\n  \\"start_url\\": \\"/index.html\\",  \\n  \\"display\\": \\"standalone\\",  \\n  \\"background_color\\": \\"#ffffff\\",  \\n  \\"theme_color\\": \\"#000000\\",  \\n  \\"icons\\": [    \\n    {      \\n      \\"src\\": \\"/images/icon-192x192.png\\",      \\n      \\"sizes\\": \\"192x192\\",      \\n      \\"type\\": \\"image/png\\"    \\n    }  \\n  ] \\n}"],[57,"A dark theme can be implemented with the CSS media query `prefers-color-scheme`, which detects the user\'s preference as to the colour scheme (light or dark). The appropriate styles are applied depending on the browser\'s preference. You can also set up manual theme switching with JavaScript and CSS classes.","Support for a dark theme in web applications lets users switch between the light and dark themes automatically based on their system settings. This is achieved with the CSS media query `prefers-color-scheme`. This media query checks which colour scheme the user\'s system prefers (light or dark) and applies the appropriate styles.\\nAn example of using the media query for a dark theme:\\n\\nThis lets the dark theme be applied automatically when the user\'s device is set to a dark colour scheme.\\nFor manual switching between themes with a button you can use JavaScript. For example, you can keep the theme\'s state in `localStorage` and change classes in the DOM dynamically.\\nAn example with JavaScript for switching the theme by hand:\\n\\nWith this code you can save the user\'s choice and apply it on their next visit to the page.\\nYou can also use meta tags such as `theme-color` to change the browser\'s colour scheme:\\n\\nThese meta tags configure the colour scheme of the browser\'s interface according to the theme. Support for a dark theme makes web applications more adaptive and improves the user experience.","/* The default style (the light theme) */ \\nbody {  \\n  background-color: white;  \\n  color: black; \\n} \\n/* The dark theme */ \\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {  \\n  body {    \\n    background-color: black;    \\n    color: white;  \\n  } \\n}\\n\\nconst toggleTheme = () => {  \\n  const theme = document.body.classList.toggle(\'dark\') ? \'dark\' : \'light\';    \\n  localStorage.setItem(\'theme\', theme); \\n}; \\n  \\ndocument.addEventListener(\'DOMContentLoaded\', () => {  \\n  const savedTheme = localStorage.getItem(\'theme\');  \\n  if (savedTheme) {    \\n    document.body.classList.add(savedTheme);  \\n  } \\n});\\n\\n<meta name=\\"theme-color\\" content=\\"#000000\\" media=\\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\\"> \\n<meta name=\\"theme-color\\" content=\\"#ffffff\\" media=\\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\\">"],[58,"Optimizing images involves several methods: compressing images (using the WebP, JPEG or PNG formats), loading images only as they are needed (lazy loading), choosing responsive images of different sizes for different devices, using a CDN for fast delivery, and adding the `alt` attribute to improve accessibility.","Optimizing images is an important aspect of increasing a site\'s performance. Several key methods include:\\nCompressing images: using image compression tools (TinyPNG, for example) reduces their size without any noticeable loss of quality. For web pages it is recommended to use modern image formats such as WebP, which provide better compression than traditional formats such as JPEG or PNG.\\nResponsive images: you can provide images of different sizes for different screens using the `srcset` attribute:\\n\\nThis lets images matching the device\'s resolution be loaded, saving data.\\nLazy loading: images are loaded only when they come into the user\'s view. This can be done with the `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute:\\n\\nUsing a CDN: keeping images on a Content Delivery Network (CDN) lets them be delivered faster, since the requests will be handled by the nearest servers.\\nOptimizing through CSS and SVG: sometimes raster images can be replaced with vector ones (SVG), especially for icons and simple graphics.\\nThese methods help reduce a page\'s loading time considerably and improve SEO.","<img src=\\"small.jpg\\" srcset=\\"large.jpg 1024w, medium.jpg 640w, small.jpg 320w\\" alt=\\"Example Image\\">\\n\\n<img src=\\"image.jpg\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"Example Image\\">"],[59,"Web accessibility means that websites and applications should be usable by people with various impairments, such as impairments of vision, hearing or mobility. It matters because it makes the internet available to every user, including those who use special devices such as screen readers.","Web accessibility is the concept of building websites and applications that can be used by people with various impairments, including impairments of vision, hearing, mobility and cognitive function. The principles of web accessibility involve adapting interfaces to work with assistive technologies such as screen readers, and ensuring that every element of a web page is reachable by keyboard.\\nWhy does this matter?\\n\\n- Inclusivity: around 15 % of the world\'s population has some form of disability. Making websites accessible makes the internet more inclusive for every user.\\n\\n- Legislation: many countries have laws and standards (the WCAG, for example) requiring the principles of web accessibility to be observed. Failing to meet those standards can have legal consequences.\\n\\n- Better performance: building an accessible site often improves overall performance and the user experience, since such sites tend to be better structured and more convenient to use.\\n\\nThe main accessibility practices:\\n\\n- Alternative text for images: adding `alt` text to images so that screen readers can describe them to people with visual impairments.\\n\\n- Focusable elements: make sure every interactive element is reachable by keyboard (buttons, links, for example).\\n\\n- Colour contrast: using sufficient contrast between text and background to improve readability.\\n\\nAn example of a simple accessibility improvement:\\n\\nAccessibility on the internet makes it convenient for every user, including people with impairments, and helps you create better, more inclusive products.","<img src=\\"image.jpg\\" alt=\\"A description of the image\\">"],[60,"The key metrics for assessing a website\'s performance include First Contentful Paint (FCP), which measures when content is first displayed; Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), which measures when the page\'s main content becomes visible; First Input Delay (FID), which shows how quickly the site responds to the user\'s first interaction; and Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS), which measures the stability of the page\'s layout. These metrics help you assess how quickly and how stably a website loads.","To assess a website\'s performance, developers use several key metrics that help measure how quickly and efficiently a page loads and responds to the user\'s actions. Here are the main metrics that are applied most often:\\n\\n- First Contentful Paint (FCP): FCP measures the time at which the browser starts rendering the first element on the page (text, an image, a block). This matters because it shows when the user sees the first visual changes on the page. The faster the FCP, the better the user perceives the site\'s loading.\\n\\n- Largest Contentful Paint (LCP): LCP measures the time it takes for the page\'s main content to become visible (a large image or block of text, for example). A good LCP is an indicator that the important content loads quickly. The metric helps you understand how long the user has to wait before the main information appears.\\n\\n- First Input Delay (FID): FID measures the time from the user\'s first interaction (pressing a button, for example) to the moment the browser responds to that action. A high FID can be caused by heavy JavaScript operations blocking the interface. Improving FID makes a site more responsive.\\n\\n- Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS): CLS measures the stability of the page\'s layout as it loads. If elements move unexpectedly during loading (because fonts or images load late, for example), that creates a negative experience for the user. A good CLS shows that the page does not \\"jump\\" during rendering.\\n\\nAn example of CLS values:\\n\\nThese metrics form the basis for assessing the user experience and serve as reference points for improving a site\'s performance, which makes it faster and more convenient for users.","<style>\\n  img { width: 100%; height: auto; } /* Stating the dimensions prevents jumps */ \\n</style>"],[61,"Critical CSS is the set of styles needed for the page\'s initial display, which are inlined into the HTML in order to avoid a loading delay. The remaining styles are loaded asynchronously. The lazy loading of styles involves using JavaScript to attach or load CSS conditionally, which lets styles be loaded as they are needed.","Critical CSS consists of the styles needed to display the content that is \\"above the fold\\", and it is inlined directly into the HTML document in order to speed up the first paint.\\nAfter that the remaining styles can be loaded asynchronously, using the `rel=\\"preload\\"` attribute or `media=\\"print\\"` followed by a change to `media=\\"all\\"`, so as to postpone their application until the page has loaded. This prevents the main page\'s painting from being blocked and, once loading is complete, lets the styles become active for every device.\\nThe lazy loading of styles lets you attach CSS files as they are needed: with JavaScript, for example, to load styles when the user scrolls to a particular section, or using the `media` attribute to attach styles when a given condition is met.\\nYou can also apply bundlers and tools such as `Webpack` and `critical`, which extract the critical styles automatically and simplify the optimization process.\\nAn example of using lazy loading:\\n\\nIn this example the styles load asynchronously, since `media=\\"print\\"` initially prevents them from being applied to the screen display. Once loading is complete the attribute is changed to `media=\\"all\\"` and the styles start applying to every kind of device.","<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\" media=\\"print\\" onload=\\"this.media=\'all\'\\">"],[62,"PHP is used to generate HTML5 content dynamically, including creating multimedia elements, working with localStorage and handling forms. PHP can generate HTML5 elements such as forms and video or audio tags, and pass data into JavaScript for working with new HTML5 features such as local storage.","PHP can work with HTML5 by generating dynamic content on the server side, which is then used to enhance the client side with HTML5\'s new capabilities. Here are several examples:\\nCreating multimedia elements dynamically: PHP can generate video and audio tags for HTML5. For example:\\n\\nInteractivity with localStorage: PHP can pass data into JavaScript, which then uses the HTML5 API to save the data in `localStorage`. An example of PHP and JavaScript:\\n\\nHandling HTML5 forms: PHP handles forms with the new HTML5 fields (`email`, `tel`, for example), performing server-side validation and providing the results. For example:\\n\\nPHP works conveniently with HTML5\'s new capabilities, providing dynamic content generation and the handling of forms with additional features.","echo \\"<video controls>\\n         <source src=\'video.mp4\' type=\'video/mp4\'>\\n         Your browser does not support the video tag.\\n       </video>\\";\\n\\necho \\"<script>\\n        localStorage.setItem(\'user\', \'JohnDoe\');\\n        alert(localStorage.getItem(\'user\'));\\n      <\/script>\\";\\n\\nif ($_SERVER[\'REQUEST_METHOD\'] === \'POST\') {\\n      // Checking the form\'s data\\n      if (filter_var($_POST[\'email\'], FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL)) {\\n          echo \\"Email is valid!\\";\\n      } else {\\n          echo \\"Invalid email!\\";\\n      }\\n}"],[63,"To embed web pages an iframe (inline frame) is used. This is an HTML element that creates a separate window inside a page for displaying another document. You can also use the components of frontend frameworks or server-side includes (SSI), but an iframe is the simplest and most universal way.","An iframe (Inline Frame) is an HTML element that lets you embed one HTML page in another. It creates an independent viewport inside the parent page.\\nThe iframe\'s main attributes: src (the address of the page being embedded), width and height (the dimensions), frameborder (the borders).\\nAn iframe example:\\n\\nThe advantages of an iframe: complete isolation of styles and scripts, simplicity of use, support for cross-domain resources.\\nThe disadvantages: problems with SEO, possible security problems, difficulty managing the content\'s dimensions.\\nThe alternatives: React/Vue/Angular components for reusing UI, loading content with AJAX, Server Side Includes (SSI) for static sites.","<iframe src=\\"https://example.com\\" width=\\"600\\" height=\\"400\\"></iframe>"],[64,"Lazy loading is used. To do this you add the attribute `loading=\\"lazy\\"` to the `<img>`, or use the Intersection Observer in JavaScript.","The HTML attribute `loading=\\"lazy\\"` (supported in modern browsers):\\n\\nThe browser will load the image itself once it comes into the visible area.\\nThe Intersection Observer API (if you need more control):\\n\\nIn the HTML markup:","<img src=\\"image.jpg\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"An example\\">\\n\\nconst img = document.querySelector(\'img.lazy\');\\nconst observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\\n    if (entry.isIntersecting) {\\n      entry.target.src = entry.target.dataset.src;\\n      observer.unobserve(entry.target);\\n    }\\n  });\\n});\\nobserver.observe(img);\\n\\n<img data-src=\\"real-image.jpg\\" class=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"Lazy loading\\">"],[65,"Optimizing site loading means the ways of making pages faster. The main methods are: compressing images, minifying CSS/JS, caching, lazy loading and using a CDN. The faster a site is, the better the user experience and the higher its position in search engines.","Optimizing site loading involves many techniques aimed at reducing response time and improving performance.\\n\\n- Compressing images\\nLarge images slow loading down. Use the WebP or AVIF formats, which provide high quality at a smaller size. Tools such as Squoosh or ImageOptim help you compress pictures without loss.\\n\\n- Minifying CSS and JavaScript\\nRemoving whitespace and comments and shortening the code reduces the size of the files. Webpack, Vite or online tools such as UglifyJS are suitable for this.\\n\\n- Caching\\nBrowsers can store static files (CSS, JS, images) so as not to download them again. Configure the HTTP headers `Cache-Control` and `Expires` on the server.\\n\\n- Lazy loading\\nImages and other resources are loaded only when they come into the visible area of the screen. In HTML this is done through the `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute on `<img>`.\\n\\n- Using a CDN\\nA Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a network of servers that deliver content to the user from the node nearest them. This speeds loading up, especially for a global audience.\\n\\n- Optimizing fonts\\nAttach only the font weights you need and use `font-display: swap`, so that text is displayed immediately even if the font has not loaded yet.\\n\\nAn example of code for lazy loading:\\n\\nA script can replace `src` with `data-src` when the image appears in the viewport.","<img src=\\"placeholder.jpg\\" data-src=\\"real-image.jpg\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"An example\\">"],[66,"To integrate maps, use the JavaScript API of a mapping service (Google Maps, Yandex Maps, Leaflet). Initialize the map in a component, add markers and event handlers.","Popular solutions:\\n\\n- The Google Maps JavaScript API\\n\\n- The Yandex Maps API\\n\\n- Leaflet (open-source)\\n\\n- Mapbox GL JS\\n\\nBasic integration:\\n\\nIntegration with React:\\nUse wrapper libraries:\\n\\n- `@react-google-maps/api`\\n\\n- `react-yandex-maps`\\n\\n- `react-leaflet`\\n\\nKey features:\\n\\n- Adding markers\\n\\n- Drawing shapes and zones\\n\\n- Geocoding addresses\\n\\n- Routing and directions\\n\\n- Custom map styles\\n\\nOptimization:\\n\\n- Lazy loading of maps\\n\\n- Clustering markers\\n\\n- Optimizing performance\\n\\n- Caching requests","<div id=\\"map\\" style=\\"width: 100%; height: 400px;\\"></div>\\n\\n<script>\\n  const map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById(\'map\'), {\\n    center: { lat: 55.76, lng: 37.64 },\\n    zoom: 10\\n  });\\n  \\n  new google.maps.Marker({\\n    position: { lat: 55.76, lng: 37.64 },\\n    map: map,\\n    title: \'Hello World!\'\\n  });\\n<\/script>"],[67,"A reflow happens when a page\'s structure or the dimensions of elements change and the browser has to recalculate the layout. A repaint is triggered when the appearance changes (a colour, for example) while the dimensions stay the same. Frequent reflows slow a page down, so people try to minimize them — by grouping DOM changes together or using `classList` rather than changing styles one at a time, for example.","What a reflow and a repaint are\\n\\n- A reflow is the recalculation of elements\' geometry: dimensions, positions, structure. For example, when a block\'s width changes or a new element is added.\\n\\n- A repaint is the redrawing of the appearance without recalculating positions. For example, changing a background or a text colour.\\n\\nActions that cause a reflow\\n\\n- Changing an element\'s dimensions (`width`, `height`, `margin`, `padding`).\\n\\n- Adding or removing nodes in the DOM.\\n\\n- Using methods such as `offsetWidth` or `getComputedStyle` (they force the browser to compute the current state).\\n\\n- Changing fonts or the text content.\\n\\nHow to avoid frequent reflows\\n\\n- Group several changes together:\\n\\n- Use `classList.add/remove` rather than changing each style separately.\\n\\n- Work with an element outside the DOM (through a `documentFragment`) and then insert it whole.\\n\\n- Use `requestAnimationFrame` for animations.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA reflow is an expensive operation, and it is worth minimizing when working with dynamic content. The less the browser has to recalculate the layout of elements, the faster the page works.","element.style.cssText = \\"width: 100px; height: 100px; color: red;\\";"],[68,"After receiving the HTML the browser starts parsing it and building the `DOM`. As it parses, it finds resources (CSS, JS, images, fonts) and starts loading them. Once the CSS has loaded and been parsed, the `CSSOM` is built, and then the render tree is assembled from `DOM + CSSOM`. Next the browser calculates the dimensions and positions of the elements (layout) and draws them (paint), and then it may \\"glue\\" the layers together (composite). Scripts and styles can block some of these steps, which is why the first paint is postponed.","After the browser has received the HTML it goes through several stages, and some of them run in parallel.\\n1) Parsing the HTML and building the DOM\\nThe browser reads the HTML as a stream and builds a tree of nodes.\\n\\n- What it does: tokenization → parsing → creating the `DOM`.\\n\\n- A side effect: as it reads the HTML the browser \\"finds\\" external resources:\\n\\n- `<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"...\\">`\\n\\n- `<script src=\\"...\\">`\\n\\n- `<img src=\\"...\\">`\\n\\n- fonts from `@font-face` and so on.\\n\\n2) Loading resources in parallel\\nWhile the `DOM` is being built, the browser tries to fetch in advance whatever will be needed for the render.\\n\\n- The preload scanner: the browser can scan the HTML in advance and start downloads even before the DOM has been built fully.\\n\\n- Priority: CSS and critical resources normally get a higher priority than, say, \\"decorative\\" pictures.\\n\\n3) Building the CSSOM and applying the styles\\nOnce the CSS has loaded, the browser parses it into a structure of rules.\\n\\n- Definition: the CSSOM is the CSS object model, a representation of the styles as a tree or set of structures convenient for computing the final styles.\\n\\n- Until the `CSSOM` is ready, the browser normally does not perform a full first paint (so as not to show the page without styles).\\n\\n4) Assembling the render tree\\nNext the browser combines the information from the `DOM` and the `CSSOM`.\\n\\n- Definition: the render tree is the tree of elements that actually take part in the visual display (something with `display: none`, for example, does not get in).\\n\\n- At this step the \\"final\\" computed styles are worked out for the displayed nodes.\\n\\n5) Layout (calculating the geometry)\\nThe browser determines where the elements will be and what size they will have.\\n\\n- computing dimensions, coordinates and line breaks\\n\\n- taking fonts, container sizes, flex/grid and so on into account\\n\\n6) Paint and composite\\nThe browser draws the pixels and, where necessary, glues the layers together.\\n\\n- Paint: drawing text, backgrounds, borders, shadows and so on.\\n\\n- Composite: assembling the layers (with `transform` or `opacity`, for example) into the final picture on the screen.\\n\\nA small example: why this matters to a frontend developer\\nIf you put a heavy `<script>` in the `<head>` without `defer`/`async`, it can stop the HTML being parsed and delay the appearance of the content:\\n\\nConclusion: after receiving the HTML the browser builds the `DOM`, loads and parses the CSS into the `CSSOM`, assembles the `render tree`, performs `layout` and then `paint`/`composite`. Understanding these stages helps you explain \\"why the page is slow to show its content\\".","<head>\\n  <script src=\\"/heavy.js\\"><\/script>\\n</head>"],[69,"The critical rendering path is the sequence of steps and resources the browser needs in order to show a page to the user. It normally involves building the `DOM` and the `CSSOM`, creating the `render tree`, `layout` and `paint`. If there are blocking resources on the path (often CSS and synchronous JS), the browser postpones the first paint. Optimizing the critical path means reducing the volume and number of blocking resources and moving non-critical downloads later.","The critical rendering path describes the \\"minimum necessary chain\\" without which the browser cannot paint the first screen.\\nDefinition\\nDefinition: the critical rendering path is the set of operations and dependencies (resources plus steps) that have to complete before the browser can perform its first paint.\\nWhat the path usually consists of\\nBefore the first paint the browser normally needs to:\\n\\n- Build the `DOM` from the HTML.\\n\\n- Load and parse the CSS → build the `CSSOM`.\\n\\n- Assemble the `render tree` from `DOM + CSSOM`.\\n\\n- Perform `layout` (the geometry).\\n\\n- Perform `paint` (the drawing), and sometimes `composite`.\\n\\nWhat most often \\"blocks\\" the critical path\\n\\n- CSS as render-blocking\\n\\n- Until the styles are ready, the browser is reluctant to draw the page \\"bare\\".\\n\\n- Synchronous JS in the wrong place\\n\\n- A `<script>` without `defer`/`async` can stop the HTML being parsed.\\n\\n- A script may request styles or dimensions (`getComputedStyle`, `offsetHeight`) and force the browser to \\"catch up\\" on its calculations synchronously.\\n\\nHow people normally optimize it (the idea, with no \\"magic\\")\\n\\n- They reduce and split the CSS:\\n\\n- the critical styles for the first screen come sooner,\\n\\n- the rest comes later.\\n\\n- They make scripts non-blocking:\\n\\n- `defer` for most scripts,\\n\\n- `async` for independent ones.\\n\\n- They postpone non-critical resources:\\n\\n- images below the first screen (`loading=\\"lazy\\"`),\\n\\n- analytics and widgets after the first render.\\n\\nA small example: `defer` as a typical optimization\\n\\n`defer` lets the browser carry on building the `DOM`, and the script runs later, once the HTML has been parsed.\\nConclusion: the critical rendering path is what affects how quickly the first useful content appears. Optimizing it almost always comes down to reducing blocking CSS/JS and moving secondary things to \\"after the first screen\\".","<script src=\\"/app.js\\" defer><\/script>"],[70,"The `DOM` is built as the HTML is parsed — as a stream, from the top down. The `CSSOM` is built after the CSS files the browser finds in the HTML have been loaded and parsed. These processes can run in parallel: the DOM carries on being built while the CSS is downloading. To create the `render tree` and perform a correct first paint, however, the `CSSOM` normally has to be ready, which is why styles are considered render-blocking.","It is important to separate \\"building\\" from \\"being able to paint\\".\\n1) The DOM: as a stream, while reading the HTML\\nThe browser reads the HTML and adds nodes to the `DOM` immediately.\\n\\n- the order: from the start of the document to the end\\n\\n- if a `<script>` without `defer`/`async` is met, the parsing of the DOM may stop until the script has run\\n\\n2) The CSSOM: after the CSS has loaded\\nWhen the browser meets CSS, it starts downloading it and then parses it into the `CSSOM`.\\n\\n- Definition: the CSSOM is a structure containing the CSS rules and making it possible to compute the final styles.\\n\\n- CSS can be downloaded in parallel with the building of the DOM, but the CSSOM will appear only once the CSS has been received and parsed.\\n\\n3) The key dependency: the render tree requires both the DOM and the CSSOM\\nEven if the `DOM` is already partly ready, the browser normally needs the `CSSOM` too for the visual tree.\\n\\n- The `render tree` is built from:\\n\\n- the DOM nodes that are displayed,\\n\\n- the computed styles based on the CSSOM\\n\\n4) What this means in practice\\n\\n- The `DOM` may be \\"almost ready\\" while painting is delayed because of the CSS.\\n\\n- Improperly attached styles (too many, too heavy, at the start) can shift `FCP`/`LCP`.\\n\\n- A script may depend on the CSS (measuring dimensions, for example), and then the browser will have to wait for the styles synchronously.\\n\\nA small example of a dependency on styles\\n\\nConclusion: the browser builds the `DOM` while parsing the HTML, and the `CSSOM` after the CSS has been loaded and parsed. For the visual assembly to be correct (render tree → layout → paint) both are normally needed, which is why CSS often affects the speed of the first render.","// requesting dimensions can force the browser to finish its style/layout calculations\\nconst h = document.querySelector(\'.card\').offsetHeight;"],[71,"The browser reads the HTML document sequentially, character by character. It breaks the text into tokens and creates DOM nodes from them. Every HTML tag becomes a node of the tree. The result is a hierarchical structure reflecting the nesting of the page\'s elements.","Parsing HTML is a fundamental process without which the browser cannot display a page.\\nDefinition\\nThe DOM (Document Object Model) is an object representation of an HTML document as a tree, which JavaScript can work with.\\nThe stages of parsing HTML\\n\\n- Tokenization\\nThe HTML code is broken down into tokens:\\n\\n- opening tags\\n\\n- closing tags\\n\\n- text nodes\\n\\n- Creating the nodes\\nA corresponding DOM node is created for every token.\\n\\n- Forming the hierarchy\\nThe nodes are added to the tree taking the nesting of the tags into account.\\n\\n- Error handling\\nThe browser can correct invalid HTML, closing or rearranging tags automatically.\\n\\nAn example\\nHTML:\\n\\nThe result:\\n\\n- `div` — the parent node\\n\\n- `p` — a child node\\n\\n- `Text` — a text node\\n\\nInteraction with JavaScript\\nThe DOM tree is available through `document`, which lets you:\\n\\n- read elements\\n\\n- change the page\'s structure\\n\\n- add and remove nodes\\n\\nConclusion\\nParsing HTML and building the DOM are the foundation of how a browser works. Understanding this process helps you work with the DOM deliberately and avoid inefficient operations.","<div>\\n  <p>Text</p>\\n</div>"],[72,"HTML provides the `async` and `defer` attributes for controlling the loading of scripts. By default scripts are loaded and executed synchronously, blocking the parsing of the HTML. The `async` attribute lets a script be loaded asynchronously and executed as soon as it has loaded. The `defer` attribute postpones a script\'s execution until the HTML has finished being parsed.","The way scripts are attached directly affects a page\'s loading speed and whether it works correctly.\\nDefinition\\nScript-loading attributes are parameters of the `<script>` tag that determine when and how JavaScript code is loaded and executed.\\nThe main loading options\\n\\n- Synchronous loading (the default)\\n\\n- The parsing of the HTML stops\\n\\n- The script is loaded and executed immediately\\n\\n- async\\n\\n- The script is loaded in parallel with the HTML\\n\\n- It is executed as soon as it has loaded\\n\\n- The order of execution between several `async` scripts is not guaranteed\\n\\n- defer\\n\\n- The script is loaded in parallel\\n\\n- It is executed after the HTML has been parsed fully\\n\\n- The order of execution is preserved\\n\\nAn example\\n\\n`analytics.js` will run as soon as it has loaded, while `app.js` will run after the DOM has been built.\\nConclusion\\nChoosing the right script-loading attributes helps speed a page\'s loading up and avoid errors related to accessing the DOM.","<script src=\\"analytics.js\\" async><\/script>\\n<script src=\\"app.js\\" defer><\/script>"],[73,"`async` and `defer` both let scripts be loaded without blocking the HTML, but they behave differently. `async` executes a script as soon as it has loaded, regardless of the DOM\'s state. `defer` executes a script only after the HTML has finished being parsed. `defer` also preserves the order in which scripts run, whereas `async` does not.","Both attributes are used to optimize the loading of JavaScript, but they suit different tasks.\\nThe main difference\\n`async` is oriented towards independent scripts, while `defer` is for scripts that work with the DOM.\\nThe behaviour of async\\n\\n- The script is loaded in parallel with the HTML\\n\\n- It is executed as soon as it has loaded\\n\\n- It may run before the DOM has finished being built\\n\\n- It does not guarantee the order of execution\\n\\nThe behaviour of defer\\n\\n- The script is loaded in parallel with the HTML\\n\\n- It is executed after the HTML has finished being parsed\\n\\n- The DOM is fully available\\n\\n- The order of execution is preserved\\n\\nAn example of use\\n\\n`vendor.js` will run before `app.js`, even if it loads later.\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- `async` — analytics, trackers, independent scripts\\n\\n- `defer` — the application\'s main code, work with the DOM\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice between `async` and `defer` depends on whether you need access to the DOM and whether the order in which the scripts run matters.","<script src=\\"vendor.js\\" defer><\/script>\\n<script src=\\"app.js\\" defer><\/script>"],[74,"Synchronous scripts block the parsing of the HTML until they have been loaded and executed. Until the script has run, the browser cannot carry on building the DOM. This slows the page\'s first render down and can delay the display of the content. The more such scripts there are, the worse the perceived performance.","By default every script in HTML is loaded synchronously unless special attributes are given.\\nWhat synchronous loading means\\nSynchronous loading means that the browser:\\n\\n- stops parsing the HTML\\n\\n- loads the JavaScript file\\n\\n- executes the code\\n\\n- and only then carries on processing the HTML\\n\\nWhy this blocks rendering\\n\\n- The parsing of the DOM stops\\nUntil the script has run, the DOM tree cannot be built fully.\\n\\n- The render tree is blocked\\nWithout the DOM and the CSSOM the browser cannot form the render tree.\\n\\n- The first display is delayed\\nThe user sees a blank page, or partly loaded content, for longer.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nThe text inside the `<p>` will not be processed until `heavy.js` has loaded and run.\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Use `defer` for the main code\\n\\n- Move critical scripts to the end of the HTML\\n\\n- Minify and combine JavaScript files\\n\\nConclusion\\nSynchronous scripts have a strong effect on rendering speed and should be used only where they are genuinely necessary.","<script src=\\"heavy.js\\"><\/script>\\n<p>The page\'s content</p>"],[75,"The browser receives the HTML after performing an HTTP request to the server. As soon as it has received it, it starts parsing the document. The DOM tree is formed during parsing. In parallel the browser loads the CSS and builds the CSSOM. These structures are used for the page\'s subsequent rendering.","Once the network connection has been established, the browser starts receiving the data from which the page\'s visual representation is then formed.\\nReceiving the HTML\\nThe HTML arrives in response to an HTTP request. The browser does not wait for the whole document to load but starts processing it as the data arrives.\\nForming the DOM\\nThe DOM is created while the HTML is being parsed:\\n\\n- The HTML is taken apart token by token.\\n\\n- A DOM node is created for every element.\\n\\n- The nodes are linked into a tree structure.\\n\\nThe DOM represents the document\'s logical structure and is used by JavaScript to work with the page\'s elements.\\nForming the CSSOM\\nThe CSSOM is created separately from the CSS files and inline styles:\\n\\n- The browser loads all the CSS resources.\\n\\n- Every CSS file is parsed into an object model.\\n\\n- The CSSOM describes which styles apply to which elements.\\n\\nThe connection between the DOM and the CSSOM\\nThe DOM and the CSSOM are combined to build the render tree, which is used when the page is painted. Without the CSSOM the browser cannot display elements correctly, which is why CSS blocks rendering.\\nConclusion\\nHTML is used to build the DOM and CSS to create the CSSOM. These structures are formed at an early stage of a page\'s loading and are the foundation for the subsequent rendering and for the work of JavaScript.",null],[76,"The DOM is the overall tree of a page\'s elements, available to JavaScript and CSS. The Shadow DOM is an isolated subtree attached to a particular element. Styles and markup inside the Shadow DOM do not affect the rest of the page. This lets you create reusable, protected components. The Shadow DOM is used actively in Web Components.","In the browser there are several levels of working with markup, and the Shadow DOM was introduced to solve the problems caused by the global nature of the DOM and CSS.\\nThe ordinary DOM\\nThe DOM is a single structure for the document:\\n\\n- every element is in one tree\\n\\n- CSS is global by default\\n\\n- JavaScript can reach any node\\n\\nThis is convenient, but in large applications it leads to style conflicts and unpredictable behaviour.\\nThe Shadow DOM\\nThe Shadow DOM creates an isolated subtree inside an element:\\n\\n- styles inside do not leak out\\n\\n- external styles do not affect the content of the Shadow DOM\\n\\n- the structure is hidden from a direct traversal of the DOM\\n\\nAn example of creating a Shadow DOM:\\n\\nThe main differences\\nThe key differences can be put like this:\\n\\n- the DOM is global, the Shadow DOM is isolated\\n\\n- CSS in the DOM affects the whole page, in the Shadow DOM only the component\\n\\n- the Shadow DOM is intended for a component-based approach\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Shadow DOM solves the problem of isolating markup and styles. It is especially useful when creating reusable UI components and lies at the heart of Web Components.","const host = document.querySelector(\'#app\')\\nconst shadowRoot = host.attachShadow({ mode: \'open\' })\\n\\nshadowRoot.innerHTML = `<p>Shadow content</p>`"],[77,"The basic structure of an HTML document includes `<!DOCTYPE>`, the root element `<html>`, and the `<head>` and `<body>` sections.\\n`<!DOCTYPE>` tells the browser that a modern HTML standard is being used.\\nMetadata goes in `<head>`, and the page\'s content in `<body>`.\\nWithout this structure the document may display incorrectly.","An HTML document has a strict minimum structure that the browser needs in order to parse and render the page correctly.\\nDefinition\\nThe basic structure of an HTML document is the minimum set of elements that defines the document\'s type, its metadata and the content that is displayed.\\nThe main structural elements\\nBefore displaying the page the browser processes the following parts in sequence:\\n\\n- `<!DOCTYPE html>`\\n\\n- States that the document conforms to the HTML5 standard\\n\\n- Switches on standards mode for rendering\\n\\n- `<html>`\\n\\n- The root element of the whole document\\n\\n- Normally carries a `lang` attribute, `lang=\\"ru\\"` for example\\n\\n- `<head>`\\n\\n- Contains the housekeeping information\\n\\n- Includes:\\n\\n- `<meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">`\\n\\n- `<title>`\\n\\n- `<meta name=\\"viewport\\">`\\n\\n- links to styles and other resources\\n\\n- `<body>`\\n\\n- Contains all the page\'s visual and interactive content\\n\\n- This is precisely the content the user sees\\n\\nAn example of the minimum structure\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe basic HTML structure is the foundation of every web page. Without it the browser will not be able to interpret the document correctly, which directly affects display, accessibility and SEO.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html lang=\\"ru\\">\\n  <head>\\n    <meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">\\n    <title>The page\'s title</title>\\n  </head>\\n  <body>\\n    <h1>The page\'s content</h1>\\n  </body>\\n</html>"],[78,"Semantic markup is the use of HTML tags according to their meaning.\\nIt helps browsers and search engines understand a page\'s structure better.\\nIt also improves accessibility for screen readers.\\nInstead of generic `<div>` elements, tags such as `<header>`, `<main>` and `<article>` are used.","HTML provides tags that describe not appearance but the meaning of the content. Semantic markup is built on using exactly such elements.\\nDefinition\\nSemantic markup is an approach to HTML markup in which elements are chosen on the basis of their meaningful role rather than their visual presentation.\\nExamples of semantic tags\\nIn practice semantics is realized through specialized HTML elements:\\n\\n- `<header>` — the header of a page or section\\n\\n- `<nav>` — navigation\\n\\n- `<main>` — the page\'s main content\\n\\n- `<section>` — a logical section\\n\\n- `<article>` — an independent block of content\\n\\n- `<footer>` — the footer of a page or section\\n\\nWhy semantics is better\\nUsing semantic tags offers several advantages:\\n\\n- It improves the readability of the HTML code\\n\\n- It helps search engines understand the page\'s structure\\n\\n- It increases accessibility for screen readers\\n\\n- It makes the project easier to maintain and develop\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nConclusion\\nSemantic markup makes an HTML document meaningful. This improves accessibility, SEO and the quality of code maintenance, so it should be used by default.","<main>\\n  <article>\\n    <header>\\n      <h1>The article\'s title</h1>\\n    </header>\\n    <p>The article\'s text</p>\\n  </article>\\n</main>"],[79,"Semantic markup helps search engines understand the structure and meaning of content better.\\nSearch crawlers use HTML tags to determine the importance of a page\'s blocks.\\nThe right tags improve indexing and can have a positive effect on ranking.\\nSemantics by itself does not guarantee good SEO, but it is an important foundation.","Search engines analyse a page\'s HTML code in order to understand what content it holds and what role that content plays.\\nHow search engines use semantics\\nWhen indexing a page, search crawlers pay attention to:\\n\\n- The document\'s structure\\n\\n- The hierarchy of headings\\n\\n- The types of content blocks\\n\\n- The main and supporting content\\n\\nSemantic tags give the search engine additional signals about the purpose of elements.\\nExamples of the effect\\n\\n- `<main>` helps identify the page\'s main content\\n\\n- `<article>` indicates a self-contained unit of content\\n\\n- `<nav>` separates navigation from the main text\\n\\n- `<header>` and `<footer>` help mark out the housekeeping zones\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nIf a page is laid out only with `<div>` elements, it is harder for a search engine to understand its structure.\\nSemantic markup reduces the ambiguity of interpreting the content.\\nAn example\\n\\nConclusion\\nSemantic markup does not replace SEO optimization, but it creates the right foundation. It improves how search engines understand a page and increases the quality of indexing.","<article>\\n  <h1>How caching works</h1>\\n  <p>The article\'s main text</p>\\n</article>"],[80,"`<base>` sets the base URL for every relative link on a page.\\nIt is stated inside `<head>`.\\nOnce it has been set, the browser uses it as the foundation for paths.\\nThis makes working with links and resources easier.","HTML has a special tag that affects how the browser interprets relative paths.\\nDefinition\\nThe base tag is an HTML tag that sets the base URL for every relative link in a document.\\nHow the base tag works\\nOnce `<base>` has been given, the browser:\\n\\n- Takes the stated URL as the base\\n\\n- Applies it to every relative link\\n\\n- Uses it for `<a>`, `<img>`, `<link>` and `<script>`\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\nThe actual URL will be:\\n`https://example.com/app/page.html`\\nWhere it is applied\\n`<base>` is used:\\n\\n- In SPAs with nested routes\\n\\n- In projects with relative paths\\n\\n- When generating HTML on the server\\n\\nLimitations and risks\\n\\n- There can be only one `<base>` in a document\\n\\n- It can affect every link unexpectedly\\n\\n- It demands careful use\\n\\nConclusion\\n`base` is a powerful but dangerous tool. It makes working with relative paths easier but requires strict control so as not to break navigation.","<head>\\n  <base href=\\"https://example.com/app/\\">\\n</head>\\n\\n<a href=\\"page.html\\"></a>"],[81,"Semantic tags make a page\'s structure comprehensible not only to developers but also to assistive technologies. Screen readers use them to navigate the page. Such HTML is easier to read and maintain. Semantics reduces the number of workarounds and improves the quality of the code. It is an important foundation for accessible interfaces.","Semantic tags describe the meaning of content, not merely its appearance.\\nWhat semantic tags are\\nSemantic tags are HTML elements that state the role of content explicitly.\\nExamples:\\n\\n- `header` — the introductory part of a page or section\\n\\n- `nav` — navigation\\n\\n- `main` — the main content\\n\\n- `section` — a logical section\\n\\n- `article` — a self-contained block of content\\n\\n- `footer` — the footer\\n\\nThe effect on accessibility\\nFrom the point of view of accessibility, semantics is critically important.\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- screen readers understand the page\'s structure\\n\\n- users can move between sections quickly\\n\\n- the need for `aria` attributes is reduced\\n\\nThe effect on code maintenance\\nFor developers, semantic markup:\\n\\n- makes HTML self-documenting\\n\\n- makes reading and reviewing easier\\n\\n- reduces the risk of errors during later work\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\nConclusion\\nSemantic tags improve the accessibility of interfaces and make code clearer and more maintainable.","<nav>\\n  <!-- navigation -->\\n</nav>\\n<main>\\n  <article>\\n    <!-- the content -->\\n  </article>\\n</main>"],[82,"Semantic markup helps search engines understand a page\'s structure and meaning better. Search crawlers use tags to identify the main content, the navigation and the secondary blocks. This improves the quality of indexing. Semantics does not guarantee a high ranking, but it creates the right foundation for SEO. It makes the processing of a page more predictable.","Search engines analyse HTML code in order to determine which content is the most important.\\nHow search engines use semantics\\nSemantic tags help search crawlers:\\n\\n- identify the page\'s main content\\n\\n- tell navigation apart from the content\\n\\n- understand the hierarchy of sections\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n- `main` points to the main content\\n\\n- `article` denotes a self-contained piece\\n\\n- `header` and `footer` set the context\\n\\nThe practical benefit for SEO\\nSemantic markup:\\n\\n- improves the correctness of indexing\\n\\n- reduces the chance of the page being interpreted wrongly\\n\\n- makes working with microdata easier\\n\\nImportant:\\n\\n- semantics complements SEO but does not replace content and links\\n\\n- an incorrect structure can make the page harder to understand\\n\\nThe connection with accessibility\\nSearch crawlers and screen readers analyse structure in similar ways, so improving accessibility often has a positive effect on SEO too.\\nConclusion\\nSemantic markup helps search engines interpret a page\'s structure and meaning correctly, improving the quality of indexing.",null],[83,"Modern projects normally support the latest versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari. These browsers cover the majority of users. Supporting older browsers increases the complexity and cost of development. The decision about support always depends on business requirements. The wider the support, the more restrictions there are in the code.","The choice of which browsers to support directly affects a frontend application\'s architecture and the technologies it uses.\\nThe basic set of browsers\\nMost commercial projects support:\\n\\n- Chrome\\n\\n- Edge (Chromium)\\n\\n- Firefox\\n\\n- Safari\\n\\nThis is because of their high market share and their regular updates.\\nWhy older browsers are not supported\\nSupporting obsolete browsers:\\n\\n- restricts the use of modern APIs\\n\\n- requires polyfills and workarounds\\n\\n- complicates testing and maintenance\\n\\nSuch browsers are therefore supported only when the client\'s requirements are strict.\\nHow the decision is made\\nThe following are normally taken into account:\\n\\n- user analytics\\n\\n- the client\'s requirements\\n\\n- corporate standards\\n\\n- the cost of support\\n\\nTools for compatibility\\nFor cross-browser work the following are applied:\\n\\n- transpilation through the bundler\\n\\n- polyfills for missing APIs\\n\\n- autoprefixing for CSS\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nBrowser support is a compromise between reaching users and the complexity of development.",null],[84,"`div` is a block element, while `span` is an inline one. `div` takes up the whole available width and starts on a new line. `span` sits inside the line and does not break the flow of the text. Vertical margins on a `span` do not work the way they do on a `div`. The replacement can change a page\'s appearance substantially.","The difference between `div` and `span` is directly connected with their display type and their participation in the document\'s flow.\\nDefinition\\ndiv is a block element intended for structuring layout.\\nspan is an inline element used to mark out part of a text or inline content.\\nBehaviour in the document\'s flow\\nWhen a `div` is replaced with a `span`, the following changes:\\n\\n- the element no longer starts on a new line\\n\\n- its width no longer stretches across the whole container\\n\\n- the element becomes part of a line of text\\n\\nWorking with margins\\nFrom the point of view of margins:\\n\\n- on a `div` every margin works correctly\\n\\n- on a `span` the vertical `margin-top` and `margin-bottom` have no visual effect on the layout\\n\\n- horizontal margins on a `span` apply normally\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nIn the second case the element will behave as part of the text, even with identical styles.\\nA short conclusion\\nReplacing a `div` with a `span` changes the element\'s layout model and can break a page\'s spacing and structure.","<div class=\\"box\\">Text</div>\\n<span class=\\"box\\">Text</span>"],[85,"`div` and `span` differ in their display type. `div` is a block element, `span` an inline one. `div` is used to build a page\'s structure. `span` is applied to mark out text locally or inline elements. Their behaviour in a layout is fundamentally different.","Despite their outward simplicity, the difference between `div` and `span` is fundamental to HTML markup.\\nThe main difference\\nThe key difference lies in the value of the `display` property:\\n\\n- `div` has `display: block`\\n\\n- `span` has `display: inline`\\n\\nThe consequences of this difference\\nBecause of this:\\n\\n- `div` always starts on a new line\\n\\n- `div` takes up the whole available width\\n\\n- `span` sits inside the line\\n\\n- `span` fits itself to its content\\n\\nThe purpose of the elements\\nMost often:\\n\\n- `div` is used for containers, sections and layout\\n\\n- `span` is used to style part of a text or icons\\n\\nThe ability to change their behaviour\\nBoth elements can be changed through CSS:\\n\\nThe elements\' semantic purpose does not change as a result, however.\\nA short conclusion\\n`div` and `span` differ in their display type and their role in a layout, and the choice between them should be a deliberate one.","span {\\n  display: block;\\n}"],[86,"Different browsers use different engines. The implementation of standards can differ. Some APIs are supported incompletely or differently. The bugs of particular browsers also play a part. That is why the behaviour of CSS and JavaScript can differ.","Discrepancies in browser behaviour are a normal part of frontend development.\\nDifferent engines\\nThe main browser engines:\\n\\n- Blink — Chrome, Edge\\n\\n- Gecko — Firefox\\n\\n- WebKit — Safari\\n\\nEvery engine implements the specifications in its own way.\\nThe implementation of standards\\nThe reasons for the differences:\\n\\n- incomplete support for standards\\n\\n- different interpretations of the specifications\\n\\n- experimental APIs\\n\\n- obsolete implementations\\n\\nCSS particulars\\nIn CSS the differences show up in:\\n\\n- flexbox and grid\\n\\n- spacing and dimensions\\n\\n- default styles\\n\\nJavaScript particulars\\nIn JS the differences involve:\\n\\n- support for new APIs\\n\\n- the handling of events\\n\\n- timings and optimizations\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nCross-browser differences arise from different engines and implementations of the standards, so they have to be taken into account and tested.",null],[87,"Safari often differs in the behaviour of its CSS and JavaScript compared with Chromium browsers. It has limitations and bugs in layout, events and the handling of APIs. Some modern capabilities appear later or work differently. This requires additional testing and workarounds. Safari remains a critical browser because of the iOS ecosystem.","Safari is based on the WebKit engine, and its behaviour has a number of characteristic particulars that are important to bear in mind during development.\\nCSS particulars\\nWhen working with styles the following problems often come up:\\n\\n- unstable behaviour of flexbox and grid in complex layouts\\n\\n- differences in the calculation of `height`, `min-height` and `vh`\\n\\n- the particulars of how `position: sticky` works\\n\\n- differences in the default styles of elements\\n\\nJavaScript particulars\\nIn JavaScript the things most often encountered are:\\n\\n- a delay in support for new APIs\\n\\n- differences in the behaviour of `Date`, `Intl` and `IntersectionObserver`\\n\\n- problems with `requestAnimationFrame`\\n\\n- WebKit\'s limitations in its optimizations\\n\\nMobile Safari\\nParticularly many nuances are associated with iOS:\\n\\n- the address bar affects `100vh`\\n\\n- limited access to system APIs\\n\\n- strict rules for media autoplay\\n\\n- the particulars of handling touch events\\n\\nDebugging tools\\nDebugging Safari is harder because:\\n\\n- the DevTools are less capable\\n\\n- remote debugging of iOS requires macOS\\n\\n- reproducing bugs can be unreliable\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\nIn practice people normally:\\n\\n- test Safari separately\\n\\n- avoid edge-case CSS solutions\\n\\n- use proven polyfills\\n\\n- budget time for Safari fixes\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nSafari demands separate attention because of the particulars of WebKit and iOS, and its behaviour has to be taken into account at the interface design stage.",null],[88,"Semantic markup helps browsers and assistive technologies understand the purpose of a page\'s elements. Screen readers can announce the content and navigation correctly when the tags `header`, `nav`, `main`, `section` and `article` are used. This makes the interface accessible to users with impairments. Semantics also improves the document\'s structure and the maintainability of the code.","Semantic markup is the use of HTML elements according to their meaning rather than merely their visual behaviour.\\n1) What semantic markup is\\nDefinition: semantic HTML is the use of HTML tags that reflect the meaning and role of a page\'s content.\\nExamples:\\n\\n- `header` — the page\'s header\\n\\n- `nav` — navigation\\n\\n- `main` — the main content\\n\\n- `article` — a self-contained block of content\\n\\n- `footer` — the footer\\n\\n2) How this helps accessibility\\nSemantics affects:\\n\\n- Screen readers:\\n\\n- they can recognize the page\'s sections\\n\\n- they let the user move between areas quickly\\n\\n- Keyboard navigation:\\n\\n- a correct structure makes moving around easier\\n\\n- The accessibility API:\\n\\n- the browser passes the correct roles to elements\\n\\n3) What happens without semantics\\nIf you use only `div` elements:\\n\\n- The screen reader does not understand the structure.\\n\\n- Navigation becomes harder.\\n\\n- More ARIA attributes are required.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\nis better than:\\n\\n4) Additional advantages\\n\\n- Better SEO.\\n\\n- Easier maintenance.\\n\\n- More readable code.\\n\\nConclusion: semantic markup directly affects accessibility, because it helps the browser and assistive technologies interpret a page\'s structure correctly.","<nav>\\n  <a href=\\"/\\">Home</a>\\n</nav>\\n\\n<div class=\\"menu\\">\\n  <a href=\\"/\\">Home</a>\\n</div>"],[89,"Offline-first is an architectural approach in which an application is designed to work locally first and foremost, with synchronization to the server happening when a network is available. To implement it, Service Workers are used to cache critical resources and intercept network requests. The user\'s data is stored locally in IndexedDB or localStorage. When the connection is restored, background tasks (the Background Sync API, for example) send the accumulated data to the server, providing the recovery scenario.","Offline-first is a development paradigm that gives priority to the application working locally and treats the connection to the server as an optional enhancement. This is critically important for mobile applications and web applications that operate on an unreliable internet connection.\\n\\nThe key components of the implementation\\n\\n- The Service Worker: a script that runs in the browser\'s background. It acts as a proxy server, intercepting network requests. Its main task in offline-first is to serve requests from the cache when the network is unavailable.\\n\\n- Caching (the Cache API): used to store static resources (HTML, CSS, JS, images) and even API responses. Caching strategies (Cache-First, Network-First, for example) determine where to take the data from first.\\n\\n- Local data storage (IndexedDB): to save the user\'s data (forms, settings, the application\'s state), IndexedDB is used — a powerful client-side NoSQL database. It is preferable to localStorage for working with large volumes of structured data.\\n\\n- Background synchronization (the Background Sync API): lets you postpone sending data to the server until the network is restored. The user can perform an action offline, and the Service Worker will send it later, automatically.\\n\\nA practical example: saving a note offline\\n\\nConsider a simple notes application. When the network is lost, a new note has to be saved locally, and when the connection is restored it has to be sent to the server.\\n\\nThe recovery scenario and handling conflicts\\n\\nRecovery is not merely a matter of sending the accumulated data. You have to allow for:\\n\\n- A queue of operations: the data to be sent must be stored in the order it was created.\\n\\n- Handling conflicts: if the data on the server changed while the client was offline, you need a strategy for resolving conflicts (\\"last one wins\\" or a manual merge, for example).\\n\\n- Notifying the user: the interface should report the status of synchronization (\\"Synchronizing...\\", \\"All data is up to date\\").\\n\\nConclusion: the offline-first approach with a recovery scenario is worth applying in applications where continuity of operation is critical (mobile applications, tools for fieldwork, editors). It improves the user experience considerably, making the application resilient to network failures.","// 1. Registering the Service Worker in the main script\\nif (\'serviceWorker\' in navigator) {\\n  navigator.serviceWorker.register(\'/sw.js\');\\n}\\n\\n// 2. Saving a note in IndexedDB when the form is submitted\\nasync function saveNote(noteData) {\\n  // We check the online status\\n  if (!navigator.onLine) {\\n    // We save it to the local database\\n    await saveToIndexedDB(\'pendingNotes\', noteData);\\n    // We register a task for background synchronization\\n    if (\'sync\' in registration) {\\n      await registration.sync.register(\'sync-notes\');\\n    }\\n    alert(\'The note has been saved locally and will be sent later.\');\\n  } else {\\n    // If we are online, we send it straight away\\n    await sendToServer(noteData);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// 3. In the Service Worker (sw.js) we handle the background synchronization\\nself.addEventListener(\'sync\', event => {\\n  if (event.tag === \'sync-notes\') {\\n    event.waitUntil(syncNotes()); // The function that sends the data from IndexedDB\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// 4. The caching strategy for static files (Cache-First)\\nself.addEventListener(\'fetch\', event => {\\n  event.respondWith(\\n    caches.match(event.request)\\n      .then(response => response || fetch(event.request))\\n  );\\n});"],[90,"To solve the problems of changing the structure of data in localStorage between versions of an application, you have to introduce a system for versioning the data schema. When the application starts you should check the stored version of the data and apply a sequence of migration functions to convert the old format into the new one. This lets users keep their data after the application has been updated, with nothing lost. The key steps are: storing the version, writing migrations, running them safely and rolling back on error.","When developing client-side web applications that use `localStorage` to save state, user data or settings, sooner or later the need arises to change the structure of that data — to add a new field, rename an existing one or change the type of a value. If you simply release a new version of the application, the old data saved in users\' browsers may become unreadable or cause errors, which spoils the user experience. A mechanism for updating the data smoothly is therefore needed.\\nThe main strategy: versioning and migrations\\nThe idea is to store a version number alongside the data (`dataVersion: 1`, for example). With every change to the structure that breaks backward compatibility, the version is incremented. When it starts, the application checks the currently stored version and, if it is lower than expected, applies migration functions in sequence that convert the data from the format of version N to the format of version N+1.\\nA practical implementation\\nConsider an example in JavaScript. Suppose we store an object of user settings. In version 1 we had a `userName` field, and in version 2 we want to split it into `firstName` and `lastName`.\\n\\nWhere it is applied and the important aspects\\nThis approach is critically important for any application that stores state on the client and is meant to develop over the long term. It is applied not only with `localStorage` but also when working with IndexedDB, file formats or even a server API (although there the migrations normally happen on the database side). The key points for reliability:\\n\\n- Idempotent migrations: a migration can be applied several times without changing the result.\\n- Error handling: if a migration fails, you need a rollback strategy (not overwriting the original data until the migration has completed successfully, for example).\\n- Backups: before a migration you can save the raw data under a separate key in case something goes wrong.\\n- Testing: migrations should be covered by tests with real data from old versions.\\nConclusion: use data versioning and step-by-step migrations when your application develops and changes the structure of its client-side data. This ensures a trouble-free update for users and spares you from having to clear their localStorage forcibly with every release.","// A constant with the current version of the data schema in the application\\nconst CURRENT_DATA_VERSION = 2;\\n\\n// A function for getting the data out of localStorage\\nfunction getAppData() {\\n  const rawData = localStorage.getItem(\'myAppData\');\\n  if (!rawData) {\\n    // If there is no data, we return the defaults with the current version\\n    return { dataVersion: CURRENT_DATA_VERSION, firstName: \'\', lastName: \'\' };\\n  }\\n  \\n  let data = JSON.parse(rawData);\\n  // If no version is stated, we treat it as obsolete (version 0, for example)\\n  const storedVersion = data.dataVersion || 0;\\n  \\n  // We apply the migrations if the version is out of date\\n  if (storedVersion < CURRENT_DATA_VERSION) {\\n    data = runMigrations(data, storedVersion);\\n  }\\n  \\n  return data;\\n}\\n\\n// The collection of migration functions\\nconst migrations = {\\n  // The migration from version 0 to version 1 (if no version was stored)\\n  0: (data) => {\\n    // We add the dataVersion field\\n    data.dataVersion = 1;\\n    return data;\\n  },\\n  // The migration from version 1 to version 2\\n  1: (data) => {\\n    // We split userName into firstName and lastName\\n    if (data.userName) {\\n      const parts = data.userName.split(\' \');\\n      data.firstName = parts[0] || \'\';\\n      data.lastName = parts.slice(1).join(\' \') || \'\';\\n      delete data.userName;\\n    } else {\\n      data.firstName = \'\';\\n      data.lastName = \'\';\\n    }\\n    data.dataVersion = 2;\\n    return data;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nfunction runMigrations(data, fromVersion) {\\n  let migratedData = { ...data };\\n  for (let v = fromVersion; v < CURRENT_DATA_VERSION; v++) {\\n    if (migrations[v]) {\\n      migratedData = migrations[v](migratedData);\\n    } else {\\n      console.error(`Migration from version ${v} not found!`);\\n      // In case of an error you can reset the data or throw an exception\\n      break;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  // We save the updated data back into storage\\n  localStorage.setItem(\'myAppData\', JSON.stringify(migratedData));\\n  return migratedData;\\n}"],[91,"JavaScript in the browser has no direct access to the user\'s file system, for security reasons. This prevents files from being read or written without the user\'s explicit permission. To work with files, special APIs such as the File System Access API are used, and they require interaction with the user (through a file-picker dialog, for example). You can also use limited storage (IndexedDB, for example) for the application\'s data, but not for arbitrary files on disk.","Browsers execute JavaScript in a strict sandbox that isolates web pages from the operating system and the user\'s file system. This is a fundamental security principle that prevents malicious sites from stealing or damaging files.\\n\\nThe main limitations\\n\\n- No direct access to file paths: JavaScript cannot read or write files at arbitrary paths (`C:/Users/...`, for example).\\n\\n- An explicit user action is required: working with files is possible only after the user has interacted — through an `<input type=\\"file\\">` element or by invoking a file-picker dialog, for example.\\n\\n- Access only to the selected files: even after a selection, the script gains access only to the specific files that were chosen, not to the whole directory.\\n\\n- Limited persistent storage: to save data between sessions, APIs such as IndexedDB or localStorage are used, but they have quotas (usually a few megabytes) and do not provide access to the real file system.\\n\\nModern APIs for working with files\\n\\nWith the appearance of the File System Access API (formerly known as the Native File System API), developers gained the ability to ask the user for permission to access particular files or directories and to preserve that permission for later sessions.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\nLimited access to the file system is used in every web application that works with files: online editors (text, graphics), data-processing tools, IDEs in the browser (CodeSandbox, StackBlitz, for example). The safe model lets users control which files a site gains access to.\\n\\nConclusion: the limitations on access to the file system are a key element of browser security that protects users. For legitimate work with files you should use modern APIs, which ask for explicit permission and provide controlled access only to the selected resources.","// An example of a request to pick a file\\nasync function pickFile() {\\n  try {\\n    // We open the file-picker dialog\\n    [fileHandle] = await window.showOpenFilePicker();\\n    // We get the file itself\\n    const file = await fileHandle.getFile();\\n    const contents = await file.text();\\n    console.log(contents);\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    console.error(\'The user cancelled the selection, or an error occurred:\', err);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// An example of writing to a file (permission to write is required)\\nasync function saveFile(contents) {\\n  const options = {\\n    types: [{\\n      description: \'Text files\',\\n      accept: {\'text/plain\': [\'.txt\']},\\n    }],\\n  };\\n  const handle = await window.showSaveFilePicker(options);\\n  const writable = await handle.createWritable();\\n  await writable.write(contents);\\n  await writable.close();\\n}"],[92,"With an ordinary call to fetch().then(res => res.json()) or res.blob(), the browser loads the whole response into memory before handing it to your code. If a file weighs gigabytes, it is placed in RAM in its entirety, which can exhaust the available resources and crash the tab or the application. To avoid this you need to work with the response as a stream, processing the data in pieces as it arrives from the server rather than accumulating all of it at once.","When you use `fetch` to get data, the browser buffers the whole response body in memory by default. This is convenient for small JSON responses or images, but it becomes a problem for large files such as video, archives or database dumps.\\n\\nWhy does the memory overflow happen?\\n\\nThe `response.json()`, `response.text()` and `response.blob()` methods read the entire response body and convert it into the corresponding format, keeping the result in JavaScript\'s memory in full. If the file is 2 GB, then before your code can process it the browser has to allocate at least 2 GB of RAM for that buffer. This can exceed the memory limits available to a browser tab and lead to an \\"out of memory\\" error, or simply to a freeze.\\n\\nThe solution: using the Streams API\\n\\nThe modern `fetch` API returns a `Response` object whose `body` property is a `ReadableStream`. This stream lets you read the data in chunks as it arrives from the server. You can process every chunk and free the memory immediately, without waiting for the whole file to load.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nHere is how you can download a large file and save it to the user\'s disk using streams, avoiding a memory overflow:\\n\\nWhere is this applied?\\n\\n- Streaming video or audio in web applications.\\n\\n- Loading and previewing large logs or CSV files.\\n\\n- Gradual processing of data from the server (the progressive loading of JSON, for example).\\n\\n- Client-side processing of files that do not fit into memory in their entirety (encryption, hashing).\\n\\nConclusion: use the Streams API through `response.body` when working with potentially large responses from the server, in order to process the data in pieces and avoid a memory overflow. This is critically important for building responsive web applications that work with large volumes of data.","async function downloadLargeFile(url, filename) {\\n  // We request the resource\\n  const response = await fetch(url);\\n  // We get the stream out of the response body\\n  const readableStream = response.body;\\n  \\n  // We create a stream for writing to the file through the File System Access API\\n  // (in real code you need to check whether the browser supports it)\\n  const fileStream = await createWritableStream(filename);\\n  \\n  // We copy the data from the response into the file as a stream\\n  await readableStream.pipeTo(fileStream);\\n  \\n  console.log(\'The file has been saved!\');\\n}\\n\\n// A simplified function for the demonstration (in reality use showSaveFilePicker)\\nasync function createWritableStream(filename) {\\n  // For the example: we create a Blob object and a download link\\n  // This is not streaming to disk but a demonstration of handling chunks.\\n  const chunks = [];\\n  const writer = {\\n    write(chunk) {\\n      // We process every chunk of data\\n      chunks.push(chunk);\\n      // Here you could, for example, send the chunk into a WebSocket\\n      // or compute a hash on the fly\\n      console.log(`Received ${chunk.byteLength} bytes`);\\n    },\\n    close() {\\n      // At the end we assemble the file (for the example; in reality you should not do this)\\n      const blob = new Blob(chunks);\\n      const link = document.createElement(\'a\');\\n      link.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);\\n      link.download = filename;\\n      link.click();\\n    }\\n  };\\n  return writer;\\n}"],[93,"The main way is to use the Streams API together with the Fetch API for processing data as a stream. This lets you read and process a file in pieces without loading it into memory in its entirety. You can also use the `download` attribute on a link or the `Content-Disposition: attachment` header so that the server serves the file for direct download. For very large files, Service Workers are sometimes used to cache parts of the file.","When downloading large files in the browser the key task is to avoid loading the whole file into the user\'s RAM, which can lead to a loss of performance or a crashed tab. The traditional approach with `XMLHttpRequest` or a plain `fetch()` loads the whole response into memory as a Blob or a string, which is unacceptable for gigabyte-sized files. Modern APIs provide mechanisms for stream processing.\\n\\n1. Using the Fetch API with Streams\\n\\nThe Fetch API can return a response as a readable stream (`ReadableStream`). You can process the data as it arrives from the server — writing it straight to the user\'s disk, for example (if the browser supports the File System Access API), or performing stream processing.\\n\\n2. Downloading directly through a link or an iframe\\n\\nThe simplest and most memory-safe way is to make the browser treat the file as a download. This can be done by creating a link with the `download` attribute or by using an iframe. The file is saved straight into the downloads folder, bypassing JavaScript\'s memory.\\n\\nThis method works if the server sends the correct `Content-Disposition: attachment` header.\\n\\n3. The File System Access API (experimental)\\n\\nIn modern browsers (Chrome, Edge) you can use the File System Access API to create a file on the user\'s disk and stream data into it through Fetch. This lets you download files of any size, writing them to disk in pieces.\\n\\n4. Splitting into chunks (chunked download)\\n\\nIf the server supports byte ranges (the `Accept-Ranges: bytes` header), you can download a file in pieces using the `Range` header. Every piece can be processed separately and then combined on the client (with the File System Access API, for example).\\n\\nConclusion: for downloading large files without loading them into memory, it is preferable to use streaming APIs (Fetch + Streams) combined with the File System Access API for writing straight to disk. If maximum compatibility is required, the method of creating a link or an iframe will do, since it hands the task of downloading over to the browser.","async function downloadLargeFile(url) {\\n  const response = await fetch(url);\\n  const reader = response.body.getReader();\\n  const chunks = [];\\n\\n  while (true) {\\n    const { done, value } = await reader.read();\\n    if (done) break;\\n    // value is a Uint8Array (a chunk of data)\\n    chunks.push(value);\\n    // Here you could pass the chunks on, into another stream for example\\n  }\\n  // Combining the chunks in memory is not advisable for very large files\\n  // It is better to use a WritableStream to write to disk\\n}\\n\\n// Way 1: creating a temporary link\\nconst link = document.createElement(\'a\');\\nlink.href = \'https://example.com/large-file.zip\';\\nlink.download = \'file.zip\';\\ndocument.body.appendChild(link);\\nlink.click();\\ndocument.body.removeChild(link);\\n\\n// Way 2: using an iframe\\nconst iframe = document.createElement(\'iframe\');\\niframe.style.display = \'none\';\\niframe.src = \'https://example.com/large-file.zip\';\\ndocument.body.appendChild(iframe);\\n// The file will start downloading\\n\\nasync function saveLargeFileStream(url, fileName) {\\n  const response = await fetch(url);\\n  const fileHandle = await window.showSaveFilePicker({\\n    suggestedName: fileName\\n  });\\n  const writableStream = await fileHandle.createWritable();\\n  await response.body.pipeTo(writableStream); // Streaming!\\n  console.log(\'The file has been saved to disk.\');\\n}\\n\\nasync function downloadInChunks(url, chunkSize = 1024 * 1024) {\\n  // First we find out the file\'s size\\n  const headResponse = await fetch(url, { method: \'HEAD\' });\\n  const totalSize = parseInt(headResponse.headers.get(\'Content-Length\'), 10);\\n  \\n  for (let start = 0; start < totalSize; start += chunkSize) {\\n    const end = Math.min(start + chunkSize - 1, totalSize - 1);\\n    const response = await fetch(url, {\\n      headers: { \'Range\': `bytes=${start}-${end}` }\\n    });\\n    const chunk = await response.arrayBuffer();\\n    // Processing each chunk\\n  }\\n}"],[94,"When the user clicks a link with the `download` attribute, the browser initiates a GET request to the given URL. If the server replies with the correct headers (`Content-Disposition: attachment`, for example), the browser interprets the response as a file to be saved rather than displayed. It then shows the standard dialog for saving the file to the user\'s local file system. This process bypasses the CORS security policy for same-origin links.","Downloading files through links is a fundamental capability of the web that lets users save content to their devices. The mechanism is triggered when the user interacts with an HTML `<a>` element (an anchor) that has the `download` attribute set.\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\nThe process can be divided into several stages:\\n\\n- Initiating the request: clicking a link with `download` makes the browser perform an HTTP GET request to the URL given in the `href` attribute.\\n\\n- Handling the server\'s response: the HTTP header `Content-Disposition` plays the key part. If the server sends `Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=\\"example.pdf\\"`, the browser understands that the response body is a file to be saved rather than displayed in the current tab.\\n\\n- Saving the file: the browser receives the stream of data (binary or text) and, following its security policies, offers the user a choice of where to save it through a system dialog.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nConsider some simple HTML for creating a download link:\\n\\nIn the second example the server-side handler (in Node.js with Express, for example) has to set the correct headers:\\n\\nParticulars and limitations\\n\\nThe `download` attribute has limitations for security reasons. It will not work if the file is on another domain (cross-origin) and that domain\'s server does not send the corresponding CORS headers. In such cases the browser will simply navigate to the link rather than offering to download the file. To download data generated on the client (the contents of a text editor, for example), you can use the JavaScript API `URL.createObjectURL()` together with `Blob` objects.\\n\\nConclusion: the mechanism of downloading through links is simple and effective for serving static files or files generated on the server. It is worth applying when you need to give the user a simple way of saving a document, an image or an archive without requiring complex client-side logic.","<!-- A link to a static file on the same server -->\\n<a href=\\"/files/report.pdf\\" download=\\"my_report.pdf\\">\\n  Download the report (PDF)\\n</a>\\n\\n<!-- A link that initiates a download through a server-side script -->\\n<a href=\\"/api/download?id=123\\" download>Download the document</a>\\n\\n// An example in Node.js/Express\\napp.get(\'/api/download\', (req, res) => {\\n  const filePath = path.resolve(__dirname, \'private\', \'file123.zip\');\\n  // We set the header indicating a download\\n  res.setHeader(\'Content-Disposition\', \'attachment; filename=\\"archive.zip\\"\');\\n  res.sendFile(filePath); // We send the file\\n});"],[95,"The main approaches are reading files in pieces (chunking) through the File API, using streams for gradual processing, offloading heavy operations into Web Workers so as not to block the interface, and applying Blob to work with binary data without loading the whole file into memory. It is also important to use virtualization to display large lists of data from a file. These methods let you process gigabytes of data without any loss of performance.","When working with large files (CSV, JSON or logs, for example) in the browser, loading the whole file into memory directly can freeze the interface or cause an \\"out of memory\\" error. Special techniques are therefore used that let you process the data gradually without burdening the main memory and the UI thread.\\n\\n1. Reading a file in pieces (chunking)\\n\\nWith the `File` and `Blob` APIs you can break a file into chunks and read them in sequence. The `slice()` method (or `blob.slice()`) creates a new Blob containing part of the original file. This lets you, for example, upload a large file to the server in pieces or process it line by line without loading it in its entirety.\\n\\n2. Stream processing (the Streams API)\\n\\nThe Streams API provides a more elegant way of processing data as it arrives. You can create a readable stream from a file and transform the data through a chain of transformations. This is especially useful for parsing large CSV or JSON files.\\n\\n3. Moving computation into Web Workers\\n\\nIf processing a file requires intensive computation (complex parsing or encryption, for example), it is worth moving it into a Web Worker. That will prevent the main thread and the user interface from being blocked.\\n\\n4. Virtualization for display\\n\\nIf thousands of rows of data have been extracted from a file, displaying them in the DOM can be slow. Virtualization libraries (react-window, for example) render only the visible elements, which speeds things up considerably.\\n\\nConclusion: the approaches described are worth applying when you work with files of tens of megabytes or more. Reading in pieces and streams suit sequential processing, Web Workers suit heavy computation, and virtualization suits displaying large volumes of data. This lets you build responsive web applications even when working with gigabytes of information.","const fileInput = document.getElementById(\'fileInput\');\\nfileInput.addEventListener(\'change\', async (e) => {\\n  const file = e.target.files[0];\\n  const chunkSize = 1024 * 1024; // 1 MB\\n  let offset = 0;\\n\\n  while (offset < file.size) {\\n    const chunk = file.slice(offset, offset + chunkSize);\\n    // Process the chunk (send it to the server or read its text, for example)\\n    const text = await chunk.text();\\n    console.log(`Read ${chunk.size} bytes`);\\n    offset += chunkSize;\\n  }\\n});\\n\\nasync function processLargeFile(file) {\\n  const stream = file.stream();\\n  const reader = stream.getReader();\\n\\n  while (true) {\\n    const { done, value } = await reader.read();\\n    if (done) break;\\n    // value is a Uint8Array with part of the data\\n    // Processing the portion of data\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// main.js\\nconst worker = new Worker(\'file-worker.js\');\\nworker.postMessage({ file: largeFile });\\nworker.onmessage = (e) => {\\n  console.log(\'The result of the processing:\', e.data);\\n};\\n\\n// file-worker.js\\nself.onmessage = async (e) => {\\n  const file = e.data.file;\\n  // The heavy processing of the file\\n  const result = processFile(file);\\n  self.postMessage(result);\\n};"],[96,"Yes, you can get a cookie through JavaScript, but with limitations. The main way is to use the `document.cookie` property. It returns a string with every cookie available for the current origin (domain and path) that is not marked as HttpOnly. Cookies with the HttpOnly flag are protected from access through JavaScript and can only be sent over HTTP(S). This is an important security measure for protecting sensitive data such as session tokens.","Web applications often use cookies to store small fragments of data on the client side, such as session identifiers or user settings. JavaScript provides an interface for interacting with that data through the `document.cookie` property.\\n\\nHow document.cookie works\\n\\nReading `document.cookie` returns a string containing every cookie available to the current page, in the form `name=value`, separated by semicolons. It is important to understand that access is restricted by the Same-Origin Policy. JavaScript can read and write only those cookies that belong to the current domain and path and that do not have the `HttpOnly` flag set.\\n\\nAn example of reading and writing a cookie\\n\\nSecurity and the HttpOnly flag\\n\\nThe `HttpOnly` flag is a critically important security attribute. When a cookie is marked HttpOnly (normally done by the server when setting the `Set-Cookie` header), the browser forbids any access to it through JavaScript. This protects the cookie from being stolen through cross-site scripting (XSS). So although JavaScript can read many cookies, the most important ones (session identifiers, for example) often remain unavailable.\\n\\nPractical use\\n\\n- Storing the user\'s preferences: an interface theme or language settings, for example, which can safely be kept in client-side cookies.\\n\\n- Tracking state: simple flags or tokens for analytics (taking regulatory requirements such as the GDPR into account).\\n\\n- Working with legacy systems: some old applications may rely on client-side cookies to pass data between pages.\\n\\nConclusion: use `document.cookie` to work with non-critical data on the client, but never keep confidential information in cookies that JavaScript can reach — use the HttpOnly flag and server-side sessions for that.","// Reading all the available cookies\\nconst allCookies = document.cookie;\\nconsole.log(allCookies); // Output: \\"sessionId=abc123; theme=dark\\"\\n\\n// Setting a new cookie\\n// We give the name, the value and additional attributes such as the expiry (expires) or the path.\\ndocument.cookie = \\"username=JohnDoe; expires=Fri, 31 Dec 2024 23:59:59 GMT; path=/\\";\\n\\n// After it has been set, document.cookie will contain the new cookie too.\\nconsole.log(document.cookie);"],[97,"JavaScript runs on the browser\'s main thread, which is also responsible for rendering. Long synchronous operations block that thread, freezing the updating of the interface. The browser uses the event loop to schedule tasks: it executes JavaScript, then computes styles and layout and paints a frame. So as not to interfere with rendering, heavy tasks have to be broken into pieces or moved into Web Workers.","The interaction between JavaScript and the browser\'s rendering is a key aspect of a web application\'s performance. Both processes (executing JS and updating the screen) happen on one thread, called the main thread. This means they cannot work at the same time.\\nThe event loop and the rendering stages\\nThe browser manages tasks with the event loop. After a block of JavaScript code has run, the browser may perform the rendering stages if necessary. The main stages are:\\n\\n- Style: recomputing the computed styles.\\n- Layout (or reflow): calculating the geometry and position of elements.\\n- Paint: filling in the pixels (rasterization).\\n- Composite: assembling the layers for display.\\nThe browser tries to render at 60 frames per second. If executing JavaScript takes longer than about 16 ms, a frame will be dropped, which leads to a \\"stuttering\\" interface.\\nA practical example and the problem\\nConsider code that processes a large array synchronously:\\n\\nWhile this loop is running, the browser cannot update an animation, handle clicks or repaint the page.\\nWays of optimizing it\\nTo avoid blocking, people use:\\n\\n- Breaking work into pieces (chunking) with `setTimeout` or `requestIdleCallback`.\\n- Web Workers, to move computation into a separate thread.\\n- Minimizing operations that cause the layout to be recomputed (layout thrashing). Reading and writing geometric properties in a single loop, for example, forces the browser to recompute the layout over and over.\\nConclusion: understanding this interaction is critical for building smooth interfaces. JavaScript that runs for a long time has to yield control to the main thread so that the browser can render frames in good time. For background or complex computation it is preferable to use Web Workers.","// This function blocks the main thread\\nfunction processLargeArray(data) {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {\\n    // Heavy computation\\n    data[i] = Math.sqrt(data[i]) * Math.random();\\n  }\\n  return data;\\n}\\n\\n// The call will freeze the interface while it runs\\nconst result = processLargeArray(new Array(1000000).fill(10));\\nconsole.log(\'Done\'); // By this point the UI has not been updated"],[98,"Cookies are small strings of data that the server sends to the browser, which the browser stores and sends back with subsequent requests. You can set a cookie by assigning a string to the `document.cookie` property. You can read every cookie for the current domain by reading that same `document.cookie` property. It is important to bear in mind the cookie\'s parameters, such as the expiry (expires/max-age), the path and the HttpOnly flag, which forbids access from JavaScript.","Cookies are a mechanism for storing small fragments of data on the client side, which the browser sends to the server automatically with every HTTP request. They are widely used to authenticate users, track sessions, store preferences (a theme or a language, for example) and target advertising.\\nSetting a cookie\\nTo set a cookie from JavaScript you assign a string to the `document.cookie` property. That string must contain the name, the value and additional attributes separated by semicolons. Important: the assignment does not overwrite all the existing cookies but only adds or updates the one you have named.\\n\\nReading cookies\\nEvery cookie for the current domain is available as a single string through `document.cookie`. To get the value of a particular cookie, that string has to be parsed.\\n\\nThe key attributes and their use\\n\\n- expires and max-age: determine the cookie\'s lifetime. Without them the cookie will be a session cookie and will be deleted when the browser closes.\\n- path: states the path on the server for which the cookie will be sent. The default is the current path.\\n- domain: lets you state the domain for which the cookie is valid.\\n- Secure: the cookie will be sent only over the secure HTTPS protocol.\\n- HttpOnly: this attribute, set by the server, forbids access to the cookie from JavaScript, which protects against XSS attacks.\\nCookies can also be set by the server using the HTTP header `Set-Cookie` in its response, which is the main method for authentication.\\nConclusion: use cookies when you need to store data that has to be sent to the server automatically with every request — authentication tokens or session identifiers, for example. For storing large volumes of data that do not need to be sent to the server, `localStorage` or `sessionStorage` is preferable.","// Setting a simple cookie\\ndocument.cookie = \\"username=JohnDoe\\";\\n\\n// Setting a cookie with parameters: the expiry, the path and the Secure flag\\nconst expiryDate = new Date();\\nexpiryDate.setDate(expiryDate.getDate() + 7); // A cookie for 7 days\\ndocument.cookie = `sessionId=abc123; expires=${expiryDate.toUTCString()}; path=/; Secure`;\\n\\n// Getting every cookie as a string\\nconst allCookies = document.cookie; // For example: \\"username=JohnDoe; sessionId=abc123\\"\\n\\n// A function for getting a value by name\\nfunction getCookie(name) {\\n  const cookies = document.cookie.split(\'; \');\\n  for (let cookie of cookies) {\\n    const [key, value] = cookie.split(\'=\');\\n    if (key === name) return value;\\n  }\\n  return null;\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(getCookie(\'username\')); // Prints: JohnDoe"],[99,"requestAnimationFrame is a browser API that puts a callback into a special queue tied to the rendering cycle. The event loop manages the execution of tasks from different queues: macrotasks, microtasks and tasks related to painting. A requestAnimationFrame callback runs immediately before the stage of updating styles and layout (Layout/Paint) in the rendering cycle, which provides smooth animation synchronized with the screen\'s refresh rate.","To understand the connection between `requestAnimationFrame` (rAF) and the event loop, you first have to recall that the event loop is a mechanism that continuously processes tasks from different queues. The event loop\'s main phases include executing macrotasks (`setTimeout`, for example), microtasks (promises, for example) and tasks related to rendering.\\nWhere rAF sits in the event loop\\nA callback passed into `requestAnimationFrame` does not go into the general macrotask queue. Instead the browser places it in a special animation-frame queue. That queue is processed at the \\"update the rendering\\" stage inside the event loop. This stage happens only when the browser is preparing to repaint the screen, normally synchronized with the monitor\'s refresh rate (60 Hz, for example).\\nThe order of execution\\nA typical cycle for a frame with animation looks like this:\\n\\n- All the microtasks run (promise handlers, for example).\\n- One macrotask runs (a `setTimeout` callback, for example).\\n- The render-update phase arrives. Here, if it is time to paint a frame:\\n- All the callbacks scheduled through `requestAnimationFrame` run.\\n- Style computation, layout and paint happen.\\n\\nA practical example\\nConsider code that animates the movement of an element. Using rAF guarantees that the position is updated immediately before the browser paints, which minimizes jank.\\n\\nIn this example the `animate` function will be called by the browser at the optimal moment for animation. If we used `setInterval`, the calls could happen at unsuitable moments (when the browser is busy with other tasks, for example), which would lead to dropped frames or uneven animation.\\nWhere this is applied\\n`requestAnimationFrame` is the standard way of creating smooth animations in web applications. It is used in animation libraries, in game engines on Canvas or WebGL, and also for performing \\"heavy\\" computation that can be broken into frames so as not to block the main thread for long.\\nConclusion: use `requestAnimationFrame` when you need smooth, performant animation or a visual update synchronized with the screen\'s refresh rate. It lets the browser integrate your code into its own rendering cycle, avoiding unnecessary computation and providing a better user experience.","const element = document.getElementById(\'box\');\\nlet position = 0;\\n\\nfunction animate() {\\n  // We update the element\'s position\\n  position += 2;\\n  element.style.transform = `translateX(${position}px)`;\\n\\n  // We request the next animation frame\\n  if (position < 200) {\\n    requestAnimationFrame(animate);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// We start the animation\\nrequestAnimationFrame(animate);"],[100,"Without requestAnimationFrame the animation will run at arbitrary moments that are not synchronized with the browser\'s screen refresh rate. This can lead to jank, dropped frames and increased CPU consumption. The animation may run too fast or too slowly depending on the system\'s load. requestAnimationFrame guarantees that the update function is called before every repaint of the screen, which provides smoothness.","requestAnimationFrame (rAF) is a browser API that lets you schedule a function to run before the screen\'s next repaint. Its main purpose is to synchronize animation code with the refresh rate of the device\'s display (usually 60 Hz, that is 60 frames per second).\\n\\nWhat happens without requestAnimationFrame?\\n\\nIf you use an ordinary `setInterval` or a recursive `setTimeout` for the animation loop, several problems arise:\\n\\n- No synchronization: timers are not tied to the browser\'s rendering cycle. Your function may be called in the middle of the rendering phase or when the browser is busy with other tasks (handling events or parsing, for example).\\n\\n- Dropped frames: if the function is called too often, the browser may not manage to paint the changes, which leads to jank.\\n\\n- CPU load in background tabs: timers carry on running even when the tab is inactive, wasting battery and processor resources.\\n\\n- Unpredictable speed: on different devices with different screen refresh rates (120 Hz, for example) the animation will run at a different speed.\\n\\nA code example: comparing the approaches\\n\\nHere is a simple example of animating the movement of an element. First, the problematic version with `setInterval`:\\n\\nAnd now the correct version with `requestAnimationFrame`:\\n\\nWhere is requestAnimationFrame applied?\\n\\nThis API is used not only for moving DOM elements. It is critically important for:\\n\\n- Canvas animations and games\\n\\n- Complex visualizations and charts\\n\\n- Smooth transitions and parallax effects\\n\\n- Your own animation libraries\\n\\nThe browser optimizes rAF calls automatically: it pauses them in inactive tabs, synchronizes them with VSync and combines several DOM changes into a single repaint.\\n\\nConclusion: always use requestAnimationFrame for any visual updates that have to happen smoothly and in sync with the screen\'s refresh rate. It provides optimal performance, saves energy and prevents visual artefacts. Timers (setInterval/setTimeout) are suitable only for tasks unrelated to rendering.","// BAD: using setInterval\\nconst element = document.getElementById(\'box\');\\nlet pos = 0;\\nconst intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n  pos += 5;\\n  element.style.left = pos + \'px\';\\n  if (pos > 500) clearInterval(intervalId);\\n}, 16); // ~60 frames/sec (1000/16)\\n\\n// GOOD: using requestAnimationFrame\\nconst element = document.getElementById(\'box\');\\nlet pos = 0;\\nfunction animate() {\\n  pos += 5;\\n  element.style.left = pos + \'px\';\\n  if (pos < 500) {\\n    requestAnimationFrame(animate);\\n  }\\n}\\nrequestAnimationFrame(animate);"]]');export{e as default};